/**
 * The Register's accounts: what the sources say of each being, what it has to
 * do with light, and where that was checked. Keyed by the being's id. The
 * register itself (beings.ts) carries the short fields every list needs; the
 * prose here is three quarters of the register's weight and is read only by
 * the Register instrument, so an entry page that names a being no longer
 * fetches every account to print a row.
 */
export type BeingAccount = { context?: string; light?: string; sources: string };

export const ACCOUNTS: Record<string, BeingAccount> = {
  "utu-samas": { context: "Attested continuously from the Early Dynastic period to the end of cuneiform, with principal cult at Sippar (E-babbar, “White House”) and Larsa. Because he crosses the whole sky daily he sees everything, and this optical fact IS his jurisdiction: he is invoked as the judge who detects the concealed crime, and Hammurabi’s stele shows the king receiving the rod and ring before him. He is also the god of the extispicy oracle, addressed with Adad in the diviner’s prayers, and the one who lets the dead be seen and questioned. In the Gilgameš epic he is the hero’s patron and opens the tunnel through Mount Māšu.", light: "Central. Utu is not a god who has light — he is the daylight, and the texts treat seeing and judging as one act. He rises between twin mountains with rays (šarūru) standing out from his shoulders, a fixed iconographic sign of him on Akkadian seals. His epithets run on nūru “light”: he is “light of the great gods”, “light of heaven and earth”, and darkness in his hymns is the place where injustice can still hide. This is the clearest ancient case of illumination as a moral and forensic power rather than a merely physical one.", sources: "ORACC AMGG, “Utu/Šamaš (god)”; ETCSL 4.32.2, “A hymn to Utu”; Šamaš Hymn (Lambert, Babylonian Wisdom Literature, 121–138); CAD Š/1 s.v. Šamaš; Black & Green (1992) s.v. “Shamash”." },
  "inanna-istar": { context: "Sumerian Inanna of Uruk (E-anna) and Semitic Ištar were originally two goddesses; they were identified by the Sargonic period, and Enḫeduanna’s hymns are the pivotal documents of that fusion — so the equation is historically real but is a syncretism, not an original identity. Her defining paradox is stated in the texts themselves: she is “the woman who turns a man into a woman and a woman into a man”, patron of prostitutes and of the battle-line alike. The great narrative is her Descent, where she passes seven gates, surrenders a garment or ornament at each, is killed by Ereškigal, and returns only by giving up Dumuzi in her place.", light: "Strong but astral rather than solar. She is the evening and morning star, and her hymns call her the light that appears at the horizon; her characteristic emblem is the eight-pointed star or rosette. Her radiance is explicitly the terrible kind: Enḫeduanna’s Ninmešara describes her melam covering the land, and her approach is described in the vocabulary of overwhelming brilliance and fear rather than of gentle illumination.", sources: "ORACC AMGG, “Inana/Ištar (goddess)”; ETCSL 1.4.1, “Inana’s Descent to the Nether World”; ETCSL 4.07.2, “Ninmešara” (Enḫeduanna); Harris, “Inanna-Ishtar as Paradox,” History of Religions 30 (1991); Black & Green (1992) s.v. “Inana”." },
  "enki-ea": { context: "His cult centre is Eridu (E-abzu), archaeologically among the oldest sanctuaries in the south, and he is attested from the earliest god-lists onward. He is the god of technique in every sense: the one who holds the me, who devises the exorcistic formula, and who in Atra-ḫasīs and in the Flood story warns his favourite through a reed wall — obeying the letter of the gods’ oath while defeating its purpose. He is the father of Marduk, and his subordination to his son in Enūma eliš is a deliberate theological move by Babylon, not an old datum.", light: "Weak and indirect — this should be stated plainly rather than manufactured. Enki is a god of water and of hidden depth, not of radiance, and his sphere is what is below and concealed. What connects him to a doctrine of light is derivative: he is the source of the apkallu who bring the arts up out of the apsû, and the giver of the me. The “eye of Ea” language in some incantations is about seeing and knowing, not shining.", sources: "ORACC AMGG, “Enki/Ea (god)”; ETCSL 1.3.1, “Enki and the World Order”; ETCSL 1.3.3, “Inana and Enki”; Atra-ḫasīs (Lambert & Millard 1969); Espak, The God Enki in Sumerian Royal Ideology and Mythology (2010)." },
  "enlil": { context: "His cult centre is Nippur (E-kur, “Mountain House”), the religious capital whose sanction legitimised southern kings for a millennium and a half. He holds the Tablet of Destinies and the “Enlil-ship” (enlilūtu), a transferable office of supremacy that is later awarded to Marduk in Babylon and to Aššur in Assyria — the office outlives its first holder. The translation of líl₂ is genuinely contentious; “Lord Wind/Air” is the traditional rendering but is disputed, and the name should not be leaned on for doctrine.", light: "Present but as awe rather than illumination. The Sumerian hymns repeatedly assign him melam — a crushing radiance that makes the gods unable to look at him — and the vocabulary is one of unbearable brightness that terrifies rather than reveals. He is not an astral or solar god and has no light-body of his own; the radiance is an attribute of his rank.", sources: "ORACC AMGG, “Enlil (god)”; ETCSL 4.05.1, “Enlil in the E-kur (Enlil A)”; Such-Gutiérrez, Untersuchungen zum Pantheon von Nippur (2003); Wang, The Metamorphosis of Enlil in Early Mesopotamia (2011)." },
  "an-anu": { context: "Attested from the earliest texts, with his principal cult at Uruk (E-anna) shared with Inanna, and a revived Seleucid-period cult at the Bīt Rēš. His characteristic mode is remoteness: he holds the highest rank and the ultimate power to grant kingship, yet acts rarely in narrative, delegating to Enlil and later Marduk. Anûtu, “Anu-ship”, is the abstract of supreme authority in the same way as enlilūtu. His name is the ordinary Sumerian word for sky, so “heaven” and “the god” are not separable concepts here.", light: "Indirect. An is the sky as a structure — the luminous vault within which the light-bodies move — rather than a source of light. He is the father of the astral gods and of Ištar in one tradition, but no text makes him a radiant or shining deity in his own right, and it would be a fabrication to give him a light-office.", sources: "ORACC AMGG, “An/Anu (god)”; ePSD2 s.v. an; CAD A/2 s.v. Anu; Black & Green (1992) s.v. “Anu”; Beaulieu, The Pantheon of Uruk during the Neo-Babylonian Period (2003)." },
  "nanna-suen-sin": { context: "Principal cults at Ur (E-kiš-nu-ĝal₂) in the south and Ḫarrān in the north; his Ur priestesses, beginning with Enḫeduanna, were royal daughters and the office lasted a thousand years. He is the father of Utu/Šamaš and of Inanna/Ištar in the standard genealogy, which inverts the modern intuition that the sun should be senior to the moon — in Mesopotamia the moon is the elder. The Neo-Babylonian king Nabonidus’s promotion of Sîn of Ḫarrān over Marduk of Babylon was a live theological quarrel with political consequences. The writing d30 is a real number-writing keyed to the days of the lunar month, not a cipher.", light: "Direct and important, and of a different order from Utu’s. Sîn is the lamp of the night; his light does not expose but measures, and lunar visibility — first crescent, full disc, eclipse — is the primary Mesopotamian instrument of time and of omen-reading. Eclipse is his light withdrawn and is read as a threat to the king. The pairing of his measured, waxing-and-waning light against Šamaš’s constant daylight is one of the sharpest light-distinctions the tradition draws.", sources: "ORACC AMGG, “Nanna/Suen/Sin (god)”; ETCSL 4.13.a, “A hymn to Nanna”; Enūma Anu Enlil, lunar tablets; Hall, A Study of the Sumerian Moon-God Nanna/Suen (1985); Beaulieu, The Reign of Nabonidus (1989)." },
  "marduk": { context: "A minor local god in the third millennium, elevated with Babylon’s political rise and installed as supreme in Enūma eliš, where he defeats Tiāmat, splits her body into sky and earth, sets the constellations, and receives the fifty names of the other gods — a text that is a theological argument, not an old cosmogony. His temple is Esagil with the ziggurat Etemenanki; the New Year akītu festival, in which the king’s legitimacy was annually renewed before him, was the state ritual of Babylonia. His symbol is the spade (marru) and his attendant creature the mušḫuššu dragon.", light: "Substantial, and largely constructed. Enūma eliš gives him four eyes and four ears and has him “clothed with the halo of ten gods”, an explicit melammu statement; his name is analysed as “calf of the sun”, and in the first millennium theological texts assimilate him to solar language. This is a deliberate accumulation of radiance appropriate to his new rank rather than an inherited solar identity, and an editor should present it as such.", sources: "ORACC AMGG, “Marduk (god)”; Enūma eliš I 90–100, VI–VII (Lambert, Babylonian Creation Myths, 2013); Sommerfeld, Der Aufstieg Marduks (1982); Black & Green (1992) s.v. “Marduk”." },
  "nergal": { context: "His cult centre is Kutha (E-meslam), and he is identified or paired with Meslamtaea and with Erra. The myth Nergal and Ereškigal tells how he came down, offended her, was compelled to return, and ended by seizing her throne and marrying her — the two recensions differ substantially in tone, and the Amarna version is markedly shorter and less courtly than the Sultantepe one. In the first millennium he becomes the god of epidemic and of the wasting summer.", light: "Present, and inverted — worth stating carefully. Nergal is associated with the destructive heat of high summer and the noon sun, and in astral scholarship with Mars. His is the aspect of solar power that kills rather than reveals: scorching, fever, drought. He rules a realm defined by the absence of light while being himself associated with a burning one, and that tension is native to the sources, not a modern reading.", sources: "ORACC AMGG, “Nergal (god)”; Nergal and Ereškigal (Amarna and Sultantepe recensions; Foster, Before the Muses); Erra and Išum (Foster); Black & Green (1992) s.v. “Nergal”." },
  "ereskigal": { context: "She is not a demon and not evil: she is a sovereign, and the netherworld is her legitimate jurisdiction, administered with officers, gates and gatekeepers. She appears in Inanna’s Descent as the sister who kills the intruder and hangs her corpse on a hook, and in Nergal and Ereškigal as the ruler who is forced into a marriage that shares her throne. Notably she receives no ordinary cult of the kind the sky-gods receive; her sphere is approached with dread rather than petition.", light: "Defined by its absence, and this is doctrinally important. Her house is described as the place “where dust is their bread and clay their food”, where the dead are clothed like birds in feathers and see no light. The Mesopotamian netherworld is not a place of torment but a place of darkness and thirst, and Ereškigal is the power of that lightlessness. She is the tradition’s clearest statement that the withdrawal of light, not fire, is what death is.", sources: "ORACC AMGG, “Ereškigal (goddess)”; ETCSL 1.4.1, “Inana’s Descent”; Ištar’s Descent (Akkadian; Foster, Before the Muses); Katz, The Image of the Netherworld in the Sumerian Sources (2003)." },
  "ningiszida": { context: "City-god of Ĝišbanda near Ur, and famously the personal god of Gudea of Lagaš, who names him as such in his cylinders and statue inscriptions. He is a son of Ninazu and Ningirida and husband of Ninazimua. His original character seems to be arboreal — the winding root — and he is represented in serpent form, with horned vipers rising from his shoulders on Gudea’s libation vase (Louvre AO 190), an image often reproduced and often misread. Like Dumuzi he is a dying-and-returning vegetation figure who spends part of the year below.", light: "Essentially none, and this should be said rather than filled in. He is a chthonic and vegetal god whose sphere is root, gate and throne, not radiance. The one astral link sometimes proposed is with the constellation MUŠ (Hydra), which is plausible but is an identification made by scholars from later astral lists rather than a claim the early texts make.", sources: "ORACC AMGG, “Ningišzida (god)”; Gudea Cylinders A and B (ETCSL 2.1.7); ETCSL 1.7.3, “Ningišzida’s Journey to the Nether World”; Wiggermann, “Nin-ĝišzida,” Reallexikon der Assyriologie 9." },
  "pazuzu": { context: "He appears only in the first millennium BCE, chiefly on bronze and stone amulets and heads rather than in narrative literature — his archaeology is far richer than his mythology. The inscription on the Louvre bronze MNB 467 has him speak in the first person: “I am Pazuzu, son of Ḫanpa, king of the evil wind-demons.” His head, with canine muzzle, bulging eyes, horns, wings, scorpion tail and serpent-headed phallus, was hung in doorways, worn at the neck and carried into the birth-chamber. His logic is homeopathic: a demon is set against a worse demon.", light: "None. There is no radiance, illumination or astral role attached to Pazuzu in any source; his sphere is wind, threshold and averted harm. Any luminous Pazuzu belongs to modern reception, not to the texts.", sources: "Heeßel, Pazuzu: Archäologische und philologische Studien zu einem altorientalischen Dämon (2002); Wiggermann, “The Four Winds and the Origins of Pazuzu,” in Das geistige Erfassen der Welt (2007); Louvre MNB 467; Black & Green (1992) s.v. “Pazuzu”." },
  "lamastu": { context: "She is the daughter of Anu, expelled from heaven for her nature — a divine being acting demonically, which is why she is written as a goddess and yet exorcised as a demon. The Lamaštu series of incantations and the accompanying amulets are among the best-documented ritual complexes in Akkadian, and Wiggermann’s study is the standard treatment. She is shown with a lion’s or bird’s head, ass’s ears and teeth, suckling a piglet and a whelp, standing on an ass in a boat on the river to the netherworld. Unlike ordinary demons she acts on her own initiative rather than at a god’s command.", light: "None. She has no luminous or astral aspect; her sphere is the birth-chamber and the sick child.", sources: "Wiggermann, “Lamaštu, Daughter of Anu: A Profile,” in Stol, Birth in Babylonia and the Bible (2000), ch. X; Farber, Lamaštu: An Edition of the Canonical Series of Lamaštu Incantations (2014); Black & Green (1992) s.v. “Lamashtu”." },
  "the-seven-apkallu": { context: "They belong to a scheme in which seven sages precede the Flood and a line of human ummânū (master scholars) follows it — the pre-Flood knowledge is complete and the later tradition is transmission, not discovery. The first, Uanna, is the Oannes of Berossos’s Greek account, a fish-man who came ashore daily to teach and returned to the sea at night; the appending of “Adapa” to his name in some inscriptions has caused a persistent confusion between two distinct figures. The seventh, Utuabzu, “ascended to heaven”. Names, order and even the count vary between Bīt Mēseri, the Uruk List of Kings and Sages, and Berossos, so no single canonical list should be presented as the list.", light: "Modest and easy to overstate. The apkallū bring the arts, the crafts and the exorcistic corpus — knowledge, not radiance — and the sources do not describe them as shining. The equation of wisdom with light here is a modern gloss and should be flagged as such; the one genuine luminous-adjacent motif is Utuabzu’s ascent to heaven, and the name Utu-abzu itself couples the sun-god with the freshwater abyss.", sources: "Bīt Mēseri III (Reiner, “The Etiological Myth of the ‘Seven Sages’,” Orientalia 30, 1961); Uruk List of Kings and Sages (van Dijk 1962); Berossos, Babyloniaca (Burstein 1978); Wiggermann, Mesopotamian Protective Spirits (1992); Annus, “On the Origin of Watchers,” JSP 19 (2010)." },
  "me": { context: "The classic text is Inana and Enki, in which Inanna drinks with Enki at Eridu, is given the me one after another, loads them on the Boat of Heaven and carries them off to Uruk — a myth about the transfer of civilisation between cities told as a theft. The lists are startlingly heterogeneous: they include the priestly offices and kingship, but also the crafts, the arts of the scribe, sexual intercourse, prostitution, the giving of judgement, and — bluntly — “the plundering of cities”, “strife”, “falsehood”. That range is what makes “divine powers” only a convention: the me are not virtues but the whole given inventory of how things are done, good and terrible alike. No agreed etymology exists.", light: "Not a light concept, and it matters to say so. The me are structural and ordering, not luminous; the tradition keeps its light-vocabulary (melam, šarūru, nūru) separate from its ordinance-vocabulary. A treatise on light should place the me beside radiance as its complement — form and pattern against brightness — rather than assimilating one to the other.", sources: "ETCSL 1.3.3, “Inana and Enki”; ETCSL 1.3.1, “Enki and the World Order”; Farber-Flügge, Der Mythos “Inanna und Enki” unter besonderer Berücksichtigung der Liste der me (1973); ePSD2 s.v. me [ME]; CAD P s.v. parṣu." },
  "melammu": { context: "Among the oldest divine and royal attributes in cuneiform culture. The key structural point is the pairing: melammu is the radiance in the object, puluḫtu (Sumerian ní) is the terror it produces in the beholder — cause and effect named separately, which is a genuine piece of ancient phenomenology and not a modern distinction. It is detachable and transferable: a god grants it to a king, a defeated god or monster is stripped of it, Marduk is “clothed with the halo of ten gods”, and Ḫumbaba’s seven melammu must be removed before he can be killed. Temples and cult-images have it too, so it is not confined to persons.", light: "This is the tradition’s core light-concept and the one an architecture of light should build on. Melammu is not illumination — it does not help anyone see. It is radiance as authority and as danger: brightness that overwhelms the eye, compels submission, and cannot be looked at. Where Utu’s light reveals and judges, melammu simply overpowers. The distinction between light-that-shows and light-that-subdues is native to Akkadian and is the most useful thing Mesopotamia contributes to a comparative treatment of radiance.", sources: "Oppenheim, “Akkadian pul(u)ḫ(t)u and melammu,” JAOS 63 (1943) 31–34; Cassin, La splendeur divine (1968); CAD M/2 s.v. melammu; Aster, The Unbeatable Light: Melammu and Its Biblical Parallels (AOAT 384, 2012); Enūma eliš IV; Gilgameš V (Ḫumbaba’s auras)." },
  "ra": { context: "Attested from the earliest Old Kingdom material onward, and dominant from the Fifth Dynasty, when kings begin to build sun temples and to call themselves sꜣ Rꜥ, 'son of Re'. The Pyramid Texts already set the king's ascent in solar and stellar terms; the New Kingdom netherworld books (Amduat, Book of Gates, Book of Caverns) chart the night voyage hour by hour, with a crew aboard the barque that includes Sia and Hu, and in some registers Heka. In the Book of the Heavenly Cow he is an ageing king whose subjects conspire against him, and he answers by sending out his Eye. His true name is a hidden thing: in the tale of Isis and Re she extracts it from him by poison, and possession of it is possession of him.", light: "He is not a god who has light as an attribute; his body is the visible daylight, and the eye that sees is figured as his. The horizon (ꜣḫt) is where he becomes effective, and the daily defeat of Apep is the condition under which the light returns at all.", sources: "Pyramid Texts; Amduat and Book of Gates (Hornung, The Ancient Egyptian Books of the Afterlife, 1999); Der ägyptische Mythos von der Himmelskuh (Hornung, 1982); Jan Assmann, Egyptian Solar Religion in the New Kingdom (1995); sign value N5 = ra in JSesh signs_description.xml" },
  "thoth": { context: "His name is a nisba, 'he who is like the ibis', built on ḏḥw, taken as an old word for the bird. He is attested from the Old Kingdom, with his chief cult at Ḫmnw, which the Greeks renamed Hermopolis after identifying him with Hermes. He heals and restores the wounded eye, reckons the epagomenal days, and in the judgement scene stands at the balance with the palette. The Greek Hermetica of the Roman period grow out of this identification, but they are Greek philosophical texts and not translations of pharaonic Thoth material — treat the continuity as real and the equation as loose.", light: "A moon god: the lunar eye, wounded and made whole, is his to restore, so he is the keeper of the light that measures rather than the light that blazes. Time itself is reckoned by that measured light.", sources: "Gardiner, Egyptian Grammar, Sign List G26; sign values G26 = DHwty and C3 as ideogram/determinative in ḏḥwtj from JSesh signs_description.xml; Patrick Boylan, Thoth, the Hermes of Egypt (1922); Garth Fowden, The Egyptian Hermes (1986) on the Greek afterlife" },
  "isis": { context: "Attested from the Pyramid Texts, where she and Nephthys mourn and search. Her power is specifically operative rather than regal: the epithet wrt-ḥkꜣw, which the site already sets as a word in its own right, is hers before it is anyone's, and in the Turin poison-tale her knowledge of Re's secret name gives her leverage over the sun himself. Her cult outlives the pharaonic period by centuries and travels the Roman world; the Isis of Apuleius and the Isis of the Pyramid Texts are continuous but not identical, and the difference matters.", light: "Her ba is identified with Sopdet, the star Sirius, whose heliacal rising after seventy days of invisibility opened the Egyptian year and heralded the flood — a light that returns on a reckonable schedule, which is why the star and the goddess were read together.", sources: "Pyramid Texts; Papyrus Turin 1993 (Isis and the secret name of Re); Robert K. Ritner, The Mechanics of Ancient Egyptian Magical Practice (SAOC 54, 1993); sign values for Q1 (As, st, ws) from JSesh signs_description.xml; the site's own src/lib/hieroglyphs.ts" },
  "osiris": { context: "Attested from the Fifth Dynasty in the Pyramid Texts and central to funerary religion for the next two and a half millennia. Abydos becomes his principal cult place and the site of an annual procession. The Amduat's decisive moment is the union in the deepest hour of the night between the ba of Re and the corpse of Osiris, from which both are renewed — the doctrine that keeps the solar and Osirian systems from being alternatives. His name's spelling and its etymology are two separate questions and both are contested; keep them separate.", light: "He is the still point the light must reach and return from: the sun does not shine on him from outside so much as unite with him at midnight in the lowest place. In the sky he is read as Orion, sꜣḥ, so the dead king's ascent is stellar as well as solar.", sources: "Pyramid Texts; Amduat, sixth hour (Hornung, The Ancient Egyptian Books of the Afterlife, 1999); J. Gwyn Griffiths, The Origins of Osiris and His Cult (1980); J. Zeidler's review of the etymological proposals (2000); sign values Q1 and D4 from JSesh signs_description.xml" },
  "anubis": { context: "One of the oldest funerary gods, prominent in Old Kingdom offering formulae before Osiris displaces him at the centre of the mortuary system, after which he keeps the technical offices — embalming, the necropolis, the conducting of the dead. In Book of the Dead 125 he brings the deceased into the hall and adjusts the balance. His epithets are precise about jurisdiction rather than grand, which is characteristic: an Egyptian god is largely what his epithets say he does.", light: "No solar or luminous role of his own, and it would be a distortion to invent one. His neighbouring claim on the site's subject is the Opening of the Mouth, the rite that restores the senses to the mummy — sight among them — so that the dead can see, eat and be addressed.", sources: "Old Kingdom offering formulae; Book of the Dead 125; sign values E15/E16 = inpw and C6 = inpw from JSesh signs_description.xml (CC-BY); Gardiner, Egyptian Grammar, Sign List E16" },
  "sekhmet": { context: "In the Book of the Heavenly Cow, Re sends out his Eye against a conspiring humanity; as Hathor-Sekhmet she will not stop, and is halted only when beer dyed with red ochre from Elephantine is flooded over the fields and she drinks it for blood. Her priesthood, the wꜥbw Sḫmt, are associated with medicine, which follows from the logic: whoever sends the arrows can call them off. Amenhotep III had hundreds of granodiorite statues of her set up around his mortuary temple and the precinct of Mut, a programme usually reconstructed as a litany in stone, two statues for each day of the year. The demon class called the ḫꜣtyw are her emissaries.", light: "She is solar light at its lethal end — the Eye detached from the face that governs it, heat rather than illumination. The uraeus on the sun's brow is the same idea in miniature: the burning that protects.", sources: "Der ägyptische Mythos von der Himmelskuh (Hornung, 1982); Betsy Bryan's work on the Mut precinct and the Sekhmet statuary (ARCE); the Sekhmet statues of Amenhotep III in the British Museum, reassessed in the published catalogue literature; sign value S42 = sxm from JSesh signs_description.xml; hieroglyphic spelling as given in the standard reference article, 𓌂𓐍𓏏𓁐" },
  "hathor": { context: "Attested from the Old Kingdom and among the most widely worshipped of all Egyptian goddesses, with a great late temple at Dendera. She is a cow, a woman with cow's horns and a disc, and a lioness by turns; the sistrum and the menit-necklace are her instruments, and their sound is part of her cult rather than decoration. She and Sekhmet are two phases of one story in the Heavenly Cow, which is why an editor should not treat 'Hathor' and 'Sekhmet' as simply different goddesses — the tradition itself moves between them.", light: "Her standing epithet is nbwt, 'the Golden One', and gold in Egypt is the flesh of the gods and the colour of the sun's own body; she carries the disc between her horns. The returning Distant Goddess, appeased and brought home, is the light coming back into relation with the one who sent it.", sources: "Gardiner, Egyptian Grammar, Sign List O10; sign value O10 = Hwt-Hr from JSesh signs_description.xml (CC-BY); Der ägyptische Mythos von der Himmelskuh (Hornung, 1982); the Dendera temple publications" },
  "maat": { context: "The one point to add beyond what the site already carries is that the word and the goddess are not two things with one name. The king's characteristic offering is a small figure of Maʼat held out in the palm — the offering of order itself — and the texts say the gods live on Maʼat. At the judgement it is her feather, not her person, that occupies the other pan of the balance. Coffin Text 1130 has the creator list making the great inundation, making every man like his fellow, and making the four winds among his good deeds, which is the same doctrine stated as policy.", light: "Daughter of Re, and the criterion under which his light is legitimate rather than merely powerful: light without measure, in this system, is Sekhmet unstopped.", sources: "Coffin Texts 1130; Jan Assmann, Maʼat: Gerechtigkeit und Unsterblichkeit im Alten Ägypten (1990); the site's own src/lib/hieroglyphs.ts" },
  "heka": { context: "What is worth adding to the site's existing treatment is the earliness and the boldness of his claim. Coffin Text 261 is a spell for becoming Heka, and in it the god says the universe was his before the gods came into being, that they came afterwards, because he is Heka. He is a member of the sun's crew and can appear in the barque in place of Sia and Hu, which is exactly what one would expect if he is the compound of which they are the parts. Robert Ritner's study is the standard corrective to reading ḥkꜣ as 'magic' in the modern, disreputable sense: in Egypt it is a neutral technology of the cosmos, practised in temples by lector priests.", light: "The site already puts him with Sia and Hu at the heart-and-tongue doctrine; the light connection is that the power is exercised by uttering, and the utterance is what makes the pattern operative.", sources: "Coffin Texts spell 261; Robert K. Ritner, The Mechanics of Ancient Egyptian Magical Practice (SAOC 54, 1993); the site's own src/lib/hieroglyphs.ts" },
  "sia": { context: "Added to what the site already says: in the Book of Gates and the Amduat, Sia is a permanent member of the barque's crew rather than an occasional figure, standing with Hu beside Re through the hours of the night. The pairing is a doctrine, not a decoration — Sia is the seeing and Hu the saying, and the Memphite theology's heart and tongue are the same distinction stated anatomically.", light: "The closest thing in the Egyptian material to light as knowing: Sia is the god of the faculty by which anything is discerned at all, and he stands where the light goes first, at the prow.", sources: "Book of Gates and Amduat (Hornung, The Ancient Egyptian Books of the Afterlife, 1999); Memphite Theology (the Shabaka Stone); the site's own src/lib/hieroglyphs.ts" },
  "hu": { context: "To add: Hu is not eloquence and not command in the military sense. The word denotes the pronouncement whose saying is its accomplishment, which is why he sails with Re and with Sia — the creator perceives and pronounces, and the world follows. In the netherworld books the pair are constants of the crew; the substitution of Heka for them in some registers is the tradition's own way of saying that the two are one power seen from two sides.", light: "Where the site treats light and word together, this is the god that stands at that joint: the utterance is the act by which what was perceived becomes visible.", sources: "Book of Gates and Amduat; Memphite Theology (the Shabaka Stone); the site's own src/lib/hieroglyphs.ts" },
  "apep": { context: "He is believed to be primeval but is not named until the Middle Kingdom, appearing in the Coffin Texts as the serpent that threatens the sun's passage; the elaborated mythology belongs to the New Kingdom netherworld books. The Bremner-Rhind papyrus preserves the Book of Overthrowing Apep, a temple ritual in which he is modelled in wax or drawn on papyrus, named, then spat on, trampled, stabbed, cut and burned — and each of his names is cursed separately, because the name is a handle on the thing. His defeat is never permanent: the point of a daily rite is that the threat is daily. Modern occult 'Apep workings' are a twentieth-century invention with no ancient warrant.", light: "He is the standing counter-term to the whole solar system of meaning: not evil as a moral principle but the un-created, the swallowing dark at the boundary. The Egyptians did not treat sunrise as automatic, and he is the reason.", sources: "Coffin Texts; Papyrus Bremner-Rhind, the Book of Overthrowing Apep (R. O. Faulkner's edition and translations, JEA 1933–38); Amduat and Book of Gates; sign values O29 = ꜥꜣ and I14 = ḥfꜣw from JSesh signs_description.xml" },
  "the-ogdoad-of-hermopolis": { context: "Four couples, each an aspect of the pre-creation: Nun and Naunet, the waters; Heh and Hauhet, endlessness; Kek and Kauket, darkness; Amun and Amaunet, hiddenness. In late texts the males are frog-headed and the females serpent-headed. Their cult centre is Ḫmnw, which the Greeks called Hermopolis after Thoth. The Hermopolitan account is one of several Egyptian cosmogonies that ran alongside each other without being harmonised — Heliopolitan, Memphite, Hermopolitan, Theban — and it is a modern habit, not an Egyptian one, to rank them or to seek the 'real' version. Amun's presence in the group and his later career as the great god of Thebes are related but should not be collapsed.", light: "This is the Egyptian doctrine of what precedes light. The Ogdoad name the water, the endlessness, the darkness and the hidden, and the sun emerges out of them — on a mound, or from a lotus, or from an egg, depending on the text. Kek and Kauket in particular give darkness a personal name, which is a strong claim: the dark here is a condition with a face, not merely an absence.", sources: "The Hermopolitan cosmogony as summarised in the standard handbooks; Hornung, Conceptions of God in Ancient Egypt (1982), on multiple cosmogonies held together; Kurt Sethe, Amun und die acht Urgötter von Hermopolis (1929)" },
  "the-akh": { context: "The state is achieved, not automatic. Sꜣḫw, the glorification spells, are literally the operations that make an ꜣḫ, and the funerary literature exists largely to perform them. The living wrote to their akhu — the so-called Letters to the Dead, on bowls and linen, ask a dead relative to intervene in a lawsuit or lift an illness. At Deir el-Medina, anthropoid busts and stelae for the ꜣḫ jqr n Rꜥ, 'the excellent akh of Re', mark a domestic cult of these dead. The class boundary matters and the sources hold it: a dead person judged in Maʼat becomes an ꜣḫ; one who is not stays a mwt, and the mwt are hostile.", light: "This is the strongest light-word in the Egyptian anthropology. Jiří Janák argues the root is bound to jꜣḫw, 'light, radiance, glow', and Jansen-Winkeln reads the original notion as the sun's efficacy at the horizon, where the light is visible while its source is hidden — so 'effective' and 'luminous' are not two meanings but one. The ꜣḫt, horizon, is the same root. Being made effective and being made to shine are the same operation.", sources: "Jiří Janák, 'Akh', UCLA Encyclopedia of Egyptology (2013), https://escholarship.org/uc/item/7255p86v; the Letters to the Dead (Gardiner and Sethe, Egyptian Letters to the Dead, 1928); Robert J. Demarée, The ꜣḫ jḳr n Rꜥ-Stelae (1983); the site's own src/lib/hieroglyphs.ts" },
  "helios": { context: "Odyssey 12 has him threaten to descend and shine among the dead unless the crew who ate his cattle are punished, and at 12.323 calls him the one who oversees and overhears all things — the formula that makes him witness to the oath at Iliad 3.277. Plato's Republic VI 508–9 makes the sun the offspring and likeness of the Good, not the Good itself. Julian's Hymn to King Helios, written at Antioch in December 362, turns that comparison into three suns and sets King Helios in the middle, intellectual rank.", light: "Total, and not metaphorical: he is the light rather than a symbol of it. Neoplatonism turns Plato's analogy of the Sun (Rep. VI 508–9) into doctrine — the visible sun is in the sensible world what the Good is in the intelligible — and Julian inserts a third, noeric Helios between them. The Greek nouns for what he emits, φῶς (phôs, light) and augḗ (ray, radiance; Greek form not verified here), are the vocabulary the entire later literature of intelligible light borrows.", sources: "LSJ s.vv. ἥλιος, Ἥλιος, ἠέλιος (lsj.gr); Homer, Odyssey 12; Plato, Republic VI 508–9; Julian, Hymn to King Helios (Or. 4 Wright / 11 Rochefort); modern commentary on Neoplatonic solar theology (KU Leuven, Solar Theology in Neoplatonism)" },
  "apollo": { context: "Iliad 1 opens with him bringing plague: dishonoured in his priest Chryses, he comes down like night and shoots for nine days. He is not the sun there — Helios is a separate god in Homer, and the epithet Phoibos means bright or pure, not solar. The earliest surviving identification of the two gods is Euripides' Phaethon fr. 781 Nauck, about 420 BC. Iamblichus' De mysteriis III.11 has the Delphic prophetess filled with radiance by a fiery divine pneuma from the mouth of the cave; the prophecy is the god's, not hers.", light: "Real but routinely overstated. His standing epithet Φοῖβος (Phoîbos) is the adjective φοῖβος, \"bright, pure, holy\" — brightness in the sense of ritual purity as much as radiance. The full identification of Apollo WITH the sun is late: it is Hellenistic and Roman, standard by the time of the Neoplatonists and the Orphic hymns, but Homer keeps Ἀπόλλων and Ἠέλιος firmly apart. Treat \"Apollo = the Sun\" as a development to be dated, never as a given.", sources: "LSJ s.vv. Ἀπόλλων, φοῖβος, Λοξίας (lsj.gr); Homer, Iliad 1; Iamblichus, De mysteriis III" },
  "hekate": { context: "Hesiod's Theogony 411–52 gives her a long hymn — daughter of Perses and Asteria, holding by Zeus's gift a portion of earth, sea and starry heaven — but names no torch, no crossroad, no underworld and no moon; nineteenth-century editors often judged the passage interpolated, though West's 1966 commentary prints it as Hesiod's own. The torch enters with the Homeric Hymn to Demeter, where she comes to the grieving Demeter with a light in her hands. Pausanias 2.30.2 credits Alkamenes with the first three-bodied image.", light: "The torches. She is the torch-bearer of the night approach, and by the Hellenistic period a moon goddess. Her light is carried, local and threshold light — a light you hold at a door — rather than a cosmic emission. That is precisely what distinguishes the cult Hekate from the Chaldean one, whose fire is cosmogonic.", sources: "LSJ s.vv. Ἑκάτη, ἐνόδιος (lsj.gr); Hesiod, Theogony 411–52; S. I. Johnston, Hekate Soteira (1990)" },
  "hekate-of-the-chaldean-oracles": { context: "This is the flagged case: a cult name conscripted to hold a metaphysical position. Her name occurs in only about five of the surviving fragments, though she may be discussed in as many as sixty-six and may be the speaker in about eleven — so most of \"Chaldean Hekate\" is reconstruction out of Proclus, Damascius and Psellos rather than direct quotation of the Oracles. Her cosmic rank is genuinely unsettled: the identification with the World Soul of Plato's Timaeus was the consensus until Dillon (1990) and Ronan (1992), and later Brisson and van den Berg, undercut it in favour of a higher, supercelestial position. Present both readings or neither.", light: "The Oracles' governing image is fire rather than light: she is the ζωογόνος (zōogónos, \"life-generating\") womb filled from the paternal fire, and the Chaldean cosmos is a graded hierarchy of fires — empyrean, ethereal, material. The recurring \"flower of fire\" belongs to this register, and translating it as \"light\" softens something that the text keeps deliberately hot.", sources: "Chaldean Oracles, ed. É. des Places (1971); R. Majercik, The Chaldean Oracles: Text, Translation and Commentary (1989); S. I. Johnston, Hekate Soteira (1990); J. Dillon (1990), S. Ronan (1992), L. Brisson, R. M. van den Berg; LSJ s.v. ζωογόνος (lsj.gr); en.wikipedia \"Chaldean Hekate\" for the survey " },
  "eros": { context: "Theogony 116–22 has him come to be among the first things, after Chaos, Earth and Tartaros, as the limb-loosener who masters the counsel of gods and men — no parents, and no light. Plato's Symposium 202e–203a makes him not a god but a great daimon, midway between mortal and immortal. The fire is Chaldean: fr. 39 des Places has the Paternal Intellect sow a bond of love heavy with fire through all things. His equation with the shining Phanes belongs to the late Rhapsodic Orphica, known chiefly through Proclus and Damascius.", light: "In the Chaldean use, wholly a fire term: Eros is fire clothing fire, the bond by which the Ideas cohere. In Orphic material Eros is often identified with Phanes, the shining first-born (Greek spelling not verified here) — but that identification is itself a late systematization and is disputed. Hesiodic and Homeric Eros is not a light power at all, and reading him as one is anachronism.", sources: "LSJ s.vv. ἔρως, Ἔρως, ἔρος (lsj.gr); Hesiod, Theogony 116–22; Plato, Symposium 202e–203a; Chaldean Oracles frr. 39 and 42–44 des Places, tr. Majercik (1989)" },
  "nyx": { context: "Hesiod (Theogony 123ff.) makes her a child of Chaos and mother of Sleep, Death, the Moirai, Nemesis and Old Age. The Orphic tradition is markedly different and gives her far more power: Nyx is not preceded by Chaos but is the immediate heir of the first principle, ruling before Ouranos, Kronos and Zeus and passing rule on; in the Rhapsodies she is daughter and successor of Phanes and continues to advise her successors by oracle. The Derveni papyrus (4th c. BCE) shows her installed in a cave delivering oracles. Hesiodic and Orphic Nyx should never be run together — and the Orphic theogonies are themselves reconstructions from fragments.", light: "Privative and generative at once, which is the interesting part. She is the absence of light, yet in Hesiod she bears Aither, the bright upper air, and Hemera, Day: brightness is born out of her rather than set against her. The Orphic oracular cave makes darkness the condition of true seeing rather than its failure — an inversion the later Christian apophatic tradition of the divine dark inherits by another route.", sources: "LSJ s.vv. νύξ, Νύξ (lsj.gr); Hesiod, Theogony 123ff., 211ff.; Orphic fragments (Kern; Bernabé); Derveni Papyrus" },
  "the-moirai": { context: "LSJ gives μοῖρα first as \"a portion, share\" and only then as the goddess — the word is an allotment before it is a person, which is why the English \"Fates\" flattens it. The number fixes at three: Κλωθώ the spinner (LSJ, ἡ κλώθουσα), Λάχεσις the allotter (from λαγχάνω, to obtain by lot), and Ἄτροπος, \"not to be turned\", whose role as cutter of the thread is iconographic tradition rather than etymology. In Plato's Republic X they sit at the spindle of Necessity, and Lachesis presides over the choice of lives in the Myth of Er.", light: "Architectural rather than iconographic, and it sits inside the Myth of Er. The souls come first to a column of light stretching through heaven and earth, straighter and purer than a rainbow, and the spindle of Necessity turns in its ends (Rep. X 616b–617c); the Moirai sit around it singing. They do not shine — they work at the luminous axis of the cosmos, which is a different and more interesting relation to light.", sources: "LSJ s.vv. μοῖρα, Μοῖρα, Κλωθώ, Λάχεσις, Ἄτροπος, ἄτροπος (lsj.gr); Hesiod, Theogony 217–22, 904–6; Plato, Republic X 616b–617e" },
  "the-nymphs": { context: "Nymphs are the clearest Greek case of divinity that is local and plural rather than personal and singular: they are sorted by where they are, not by what they govern, and the same noun νύμφη also means \"bride\". Porphyry's De antro nympharum reads Homer's Ithacan cave of the naiads (Od. 13.102–12) as an allegory of the cosmos and of souls descending into génesis, following Numenius, and explains soul-body association through pneûma, génesis and apogénesis. That is Neoplatonic exegesis three centuries downstream and not what Homer's audience heard.", light: "Slight, and worth saying so. Porphyry's cave is defined by its darkness and its two gates, and nymphs belong to water, moisture and reflection rather than to radiance. They earn their place in this register as a class of being, not as bearers of any luminous office.", sources: "LSJ s.v. νύμφη (lsj.gr); Homer, Odyssey 13.102–12; Porphyry, De antro nympharum; N. Akçay, Porphyry's On the Cave of the Nymphs in its Intellectual Context (2019)" },
  "the-iynges": { context: "The word first names a bird and a charm: Pindar's Pythian 4.213–19, of 462 BC, has Aphrodite bind the wryneck to a four-spoked wheel and teach Jason the spell that turns Medea. The Chaldean Oracles make it a rank — fr. 77 des Places says the iynges, being thought by the Father, themselves think, moved by unutterable counsels — and Psellus sets them above the synocheis and teletarchai. The two senses stay joined: Marinus' Life of Proclus 28 has Proclus break an Attic drought by setting an iynx in motion.", light: "Structural rather than luminous. They are not light-beings; they are the mechanism by which the noetic fire is transmitted downward and returned upward, which is why later theurgic practice attaches to them instruments rather than images.", sources: "Pindar, Pythian 4.213–19; Chaldean Oracles fr. 77 des Places, tr. Majercik (1989); Michael Psellus, Hypotyposis of the Chaldean doctrines; Marinus, Life of Proclus 28" },
  "the-synocheis": { context: "Technical, not cultic, and built transparently on συνέχω, \"hold together\". The second member of the Chaldean triad; Proclus makes the Synoches the second triad of the intelligible-and-intellectual gods, and the later systematizers subdivide them into empyrean, ethereal and material. Because they were also called guardians, later occult writers have been tempted to read them as protective spirits — but their office is cohesion, not defence. Much of what is said about them descends from Proclus's arrangement rather than from surviving Oracle verses.", light: "Only through the Chaldean fire-grading, where the empyrean member belongs to the highest fire. They hold; they do not shine. Any luminous character they have is borrowed from what passes through them.", sources: "LSJ s.vv. συνοχή, συνοχικός, συνέχω (lsj.gr); Perseus word-study gloss of συνοχεύς; Proclus, Platonic Theology; Damascius; R. Majercik, The Chaldean Oracles (1989); N. Spanu, Proclus and the Chaldean Oracles (2021)" },
  "the-teletarchai": { context: "LSJ glosses the singular \"founder of mysteries\" and adds, \"in plural, an order of divine beings who bring initial and final terms into relation\", citing Damascius (De principiis 96). The name is built on teletḗ, a rite of initiation, plus the -archēs element of rule, so it states an office in the mysteries and carries no mythic identity whatever. Third member of the Chaldean triad after the Iynges and Synocheis, and itself a triad of three (empyrean, ethereal, material) in the later systematizations.", light: "Through the empyrean rank only. Their business is completion — the English \"Perfectings\" renders it better than any light word would — and treating them as illuminators imports something the sources do not say.", sources: "LSJ s.vv. τελετάρχης, τελεταρχικός (lsj.gr); Damascius, De principiis 96; Proclus; R. Majercik, The Chaldean Oracles (1989); N. Spanu, Proclus and the Chaldean Oracles (2021)" },
  "agathos-daimon": { context: "Aristophanes' Knights 85 already has a slave call for neat wine to the Good Daimon, and Athenaeus, Deipnosophistae 15.693, citing Theophrastus' On Drunkenness, has that unmixed cup passed in small quantity at the meal's end and named either of the Good Daimon or of Zeus Soter. His cult form is a serpent — the house snake, and at Alexandria a civic one whose killing and cult at the founding the Alexander Romance 1.32 records. Corpus Hermeticum XII takes the name elsewhere, making him a teacher whose sayings Hermes quotes.", light: "None to speak of, and the honest answer is worth more here than a stretched one. In particular, do not import the modern occult reading of the Agathos Daimon as the \"Holy Guardian Angel\" — that identification is a nineteenth- and twentieth-century construction laid over this cult figure and over Plato's personal daimon alike.", sources: "LSJ s.vv. δαίμων (ἀγαθὸς δ.), ἀγαθοδαίμων, Ἀγαθοδαίμων, ἀγαθός (lsj.gr); Aristophanes, Knights 85; Athenaeus, Deipnosophistae 15.693; Alexander Romance 1.32; Corpus Hermeticum XII; D. Ogden on Greek serpent cults; C. T. Djurslev and D. Ogden, 'Alexander, Agathoi Daimones, Argives and Armenians', Karanos" },
  "the-personal-daimon-of-the-myth-of-er": { context: "Plato, Republic X 617d–e: the spokesman of Lachesis tells the assembled souls that no daimon will be allotted to them — they will choose their own daimon — and at 620d–e the chosen daimon is sent along as guardian of the life picked. That inversion is the entire philosophical point of the myth and is routinely lost in retelling, which usually has the daimon assigned. Iamblichus takes the doctrine up in De mysteriis IX against Porphyry's attempt to identify the personal daimon astrologically from the ruler of the natal chart: for Iamblichus the daimon descends from the whole cosmos, and theurgic rite rather than calculation is the proper means of contact.", light: "Only by way of the setting, but the setting is precise: the souls reach the column of light that binds heaven and earth before any choosing happens (Rep. X 616b), so the election of a daimon takes place at the luminous axis of the cosmos. The daimon itself is not described as a light-being.", sources: "Plato, Republic X 616b, 617d–e, 620d–e; LSJ s.v. δαίμων (lsj.gr); Iamblichus, De mysteriis IX; C. Addey and others on the personal daimōn in De mysteriis" },
  "nous": { context: "Plotinus' Enneads V.1.7 has Intellect come to be when what issues from the One turns back and sees it; at V.1.8 he calls it no novelty but exegesis of Plato, appealing to the Parmenides. The familiar title, On the Three Primary Hypostases, is not his: Life of Plotinus 4 says he gave his treatises no titles and Porphyry kept the headings that prevailed. The Chaldean Oracles use the word otherwise — fr. 7 des Places has the Father hand all things to a second Intellect whom the human race calls the first.", light: "The tradition's master metaphor for intelligible light, and the origin of most later uses. Plato's analogy of the Sun (Rep. VI 508–9) makes the Good in the intelligible realm what the sun is in the visible; Neoplatonism runs its whole doctrine of illumination off that comparison — Soul lit by Intellect, Intellect lit by the One. Whenever a later writer says \"intelligible light\" without explanation, this is the referent.", sources: "LSJ s.vv. νοῦς, νόος, νῶς (lsj.gr); Plotinus, Enneads V.1; Porphyry, Life of Plotinus 4; Chaldean Oracles fr. 7 des Places, tr. Majercik (1989)" },
  "psyche": { context: "Technical in this use, and flagged. LSJ carries ψυχή across its whole range, from breath and life through the soul of the dead to the philosophical soul; as a hypostasis it is the third of Plotinus's three (Enn. V.1). The decisive break for everything theurgic is Iamblichus's: where Plotinus holds that something of the individual soul remains undescended in Intellect, Iamblichus holds that the soul descends wholly into body and therefore cannot lift itself by contemplation alone — hence θεουργία (theourgía), which LSJ glosses as a divine work, a sacramental rite or mystery. This hypostasis has nothing to do with the Psyche of Apuleius's Cupid and Psyche, who is a character in a Latin novel.", light: "Considerable and technical, but receptive: the soul is what is illuminated by Nous, not what emits. The tradition also gives it a luminous body — the ὄχημα (óchēma, \"vehicle\"), called augoeides, light-formed, in the scholarly transliteration — which Porphyry treated as a temporary set of envelopes gathered during the descent and which Iamblichus argued was an ethereal body fashioned by the demiurge and therefore immortal. Proclus, Syrianus and Hermias multiply it into a hierarchy of vehicles. This is the direct ancestor of every later \"body of light\".", sources: "LSJ s.vv. ψυχή, ὄχημα, θεουργία (lsj.gr); Plotinus, Enneads V.1; Iamblichus, De mysteriis; J. F. Finamore on the vehicle of the soul; M. Griffin, \"Proclus on Place as the Luminous Vehicle of the Soul\"" },
  "the-angel-of-the-lord": { context: "The Hebrew is a construct phrase, malʾakh YHWH, not a name, and the texts withhold one: at Judges 13:18 he refuses Manoah, calling his name pilʾi, wonderful. Genesis 16:13 has Hagar name the speaker as God, and Exodus 3:4 has God call out of the bush the angel entered at 3:2. Zechariah 3:2 is sharper still: the Masoretic Text has the LORD say 'the LORD rebuke you', a third-person self-reference the Syriac resolves by reading 'the angel of the LORD said'. Numbers 22:22 has him stand le-satan against Balaam.", light: "He appears in fire without being fire: בְּלַבַּת אֵשׁ מִתּוֹךְ הַסְּנֶה, 'in a flame of fire from the midst of the bush' (Exod 3:2), and he goes up in the flame of the altar while Manoah and his wife watch (Judg 13:20). The flame is his mode of appearing; the texts never call him luminous in himself.", sources: "WLC/OSHB Gen.xml 16:7; Exod.xml 3:2; Num.xml 22:22, 22:31; Judg.xml 13:20; Zech.xml 3:1-2" },
  "michael": { context: "He is named only in Daniel, whose visions are usually dated to about 165 BCE and which is the sole Hebrew biblical book to name any angel; elsewhere Michael is a man's name. Daniel 10:13 calls him one of the chief princes and 12:1 the great prince, but those he contends with are the princes of Persia and Greece, not the accuser: the war with the dragon is Revelation 12:7, the quarrel over Moses' body Jude 9. Ḥagigah 12b sets him at the altar in Zevul, offering.", sources: "WLC/OSHB Dan.xml 10:13, 10:21, 12:1; pointed Pirqei de-Rabbi Eliezer ch. 4; bedtime Shema formula in pointed siddur (relbns/siddur-plus, sefard)" },
  "gabriel": { context: "Two verses name him, Daniel 8:16 and 9:21; a verse earlier, at 8:15, he is 'one having the appearance of a man', ke-marʾeh gaver, a play on the name. The muʿaf biʿaf of 9:21 is disputed, read as swift flight or as weariness according to which root is taken, and nothing in Daniel gives him wings. The annunciation of Luke 1 and the Qurʾanic Jibrīl come later; the Bavli's Gabriel is fire rather than light, calling himself prince of fire at Pesaḥim 118a as he goes into Nebuchadnezzar's furnace.", light: "His office is illumination in the intellectual sense: he is sent so that a מַרְאֶה, a thing seen, can be understood. Daniel sees; Gabriel makes the seeing mean something.", sources: "WLC/OSHB Dan.xml 8:16, 9:21; pointed Pirqei de-Rabbi Eliezer ch. 4" },
  "raphael": { context: "Rephael in the Hebrew Bible is a gatekeeper, a son of Shemaiah at 1 Chronicles 26:7, not an angel. The angel belongs to Tobit, composed probably in Aramaic about 225-175 BCE, and the book is not simply Greek: Qumran gave up four Aramaic copies and one Hebrew, 4Q196-200. He travels disguised as Azariah son of Hananiah and names himself only at Tobit 12:15, as one who carries the prayers of the holy ones before the Glory; 1 Enoch 20:3 sets him over the spirits of men.", light: "In Tobit he restores sight: the remedy he prescribes peels the white films from Tobit's eyes and the blind man sees again. Among the archangels he is the one whose office actually concerns seeing, though the text is Greek, not Hebrew.", sources: "WLC/OSHB 1Chr.xml 26:7; pointed Pirqei de-Rabbi Eliezer ch. 4; Tobit 3, 6, 11-12 and 1 Enoch 20 (reported, not verified here in the original languages)" },
  "uriel": { context: "In the Hebrew Bible Uriel is a man only: a Kohathite Levite at 1 Chronicles 15:5, Uriel of Gibeah at 2 Chronicles 13:2. The angelic office is Enochic — 1 Enoch 72:1 makes him guide of the luminaries, and the Aramaic copies of that Astronomical Book at Qumran, 4Q208-211, are the oldest Enochic material we have; 4 Ezra 4:1 sends him to Ezra. The witnesses are unsteady: at 1 Enoch 9:1 they vary between Uriel and Sariel, and the four presences of 1 Enoch 40 name Phanuel instead.", light: "The name is the light-name — אוּר / אוֹר. His Enochic office is the calendar of sun, moon and stars, which makes him the one archangel whose function is literally the ordering of light. This is the strongest light-connection in the Jewish angelic repertoire and it is genuinely ancient, not a modern colour-correspondence.", sources: "WLC/OSHB 1Chr.xml 6:9, 15:5, 15:11; 2Chr.xml 13:2; pointed Pirqei de-Rabbi Eliezer ch. 4; pointed bedtime Shema (relbns/siddur-plus); 1 Enoch 72-82 and 4 Ezra (reported)" },
  "metatron": { context: "The Bavli names him three times only — Sanhedrin 38b, Ḥagigah 15a, Avodah Zarah 3b — and in none is he Enoch; at Sanhedrin 38b Rabbi Idit grants the name lodged in him from Exodus 23:21, then refuses him even as a guide. The Enoch identification, the title 'lesser YHWH' at 3 Enoch 12:5 and the flesh turned to flame at 15:1 belong to Sefer Hekhalot, usually placed between the fifth and ninth centuries. The name has no agreed derivation; Philip Alexander suspects it was coined, like the magical Adiriron.", light: "Cordovero: וְה״ס י׳ עֶשֶׂר נִיצוֹצוֹת מֵהַסְפִירוֹת הָעֶלְיוֹנוֹת מִתְלַבְּשׁוֹת בּוֹ — ten sparks from the supernal sefirot clothe themselves in him, and it is because of the divine name lodged in him that Exodus 23:21 must be applied to him. The Hekhalot literature describes Enoch's flesh turned to flame and his body to fire at the transformation. The 'lesser YHWH' title attached to him in some Hekhalot manuscripts is the most theologically dangerous claim in the corpus, and Ḥagigah 15a is the tradition's own alarm about it.", sources: "b. Sanhedrin 38b, b. Ḥagigah 15a, b. Avodah Zarah 3b (Vilna text, disacorns/talmud-bavli-text); Moses Cordovero, Derishot be-Inyanei ha-Malʾakhim; Jastrow, Dictionary (1903), s.v.; 3 Enoch / Sefer Hekhalot and Schäfer's Synopse zur Hekhalot-Literatur (reported)" },
  "sandalphon": { context: "One passage carries him: b. Ḥagigah 13b, where Rabbi Elazar takes the wheel on the earth of Ezekiel 1:15 for an angel whose head reaches the ḥayyot, and a baraita adds the name and the five-hundred-year height. Pressed with Ezekiel 3:12, that no one knows God's place, the Talmud answers that he says the Name over the crown and it goes and rests on His head. Pesikta Rabbati 20 has Hadarniel lead Moses only as far as his fire; the prayers that turn to flowers in his hands are Longfellow's, from 1858.", light: "Nothing intrinsic in the Talmudic passage; his attributes are height, position and the crowns. Jastrow records a phrase 'the fire of Sandalphon' from Pesikta Rabbati 20, which I have not verified in the original.", sources: "b. Ḥagigah 13b (Vilna text, disacorns/talmud-bavli-text); Pesikta Rabbati 20 (reported); Cordovero, Derishot be-Inyanei ha-Malʾakhim; Jastrow, Dictionary (1903), s.v. (reported); H. W. Longfellow, 'Sandalphon', Birds of Passage (1858)" },
  "the-shekhinah": { context: "The noun is rabbinic, not biblical, built on the root of Exodus 25:8, 'that I may dwell among them'; the Targums use Shekhinta where the Hebrew speaks of God's presence. Megillah 29a has Rabbi Shimon bar Yoḥai say that wherever Israel was exiled she went with them. The familiar 'feminine aspect of God' is medieval: the word is grammatically feminine, but in the early sources it names God's presence and nothing distinct from it, and Scholem placed the hypostatic separation in the late Midrash Mishlei, the feminine power in the Sefer ha-Bahir, about 1180.", light: "Extensive and central. The Talmud's picture of the world to come is the righteous seated with their crowns, נֶהֱנִים מִזִּיו הַשְּׁכִינָה, 'taking pleasure in the radiance of the Shekhinah' (b. Berakhot 17a). In Kabbalah Malkhut is characteristically the vessel or mirror that has no light of its own and shines only with what it receives — which is precisely why she is figured as the moon.", sources: "b. Berakhot 17a (Vilna text); pointed Pirqei de-Rabbi Eliezer ch. 4; El Male Raḥamim, Yizkor (Sefaria corpus); bedtime Shema (pointed siddur); Cordovero, Derishot be-Inyanei ha-Malʾakhim" },
  "the-hayyot-of-the-merkabah": { context: "Biblical, then liturgical, then the centre of the whole Hekhalot enterprise. Ezekiel 1 gives them four faces and four wings, feet like a calf's hoof, moving straight forward without turning, with a wheel beside each; Ezekiel 10:15 and 10:20 then identify them as the keruvim of the Jerusalem temple. Pirqei de-Rabbi Eliezer sets their four wings against the seraphim's six. In merkabah mysticism they are what the descender to the chariot must survive the sight of, and Maimonides puts them at the top of the ten ranks with nothing above them but God.", light: "The most sustained light-description of any Jewish being. Their appearance is כְּגַחֲלֵי אֵשׁ בֹּעֲרוֹת, 'like burning coals of fire', with torches moving among them, brightness to the fire, and lightning going out of it (Ezek 1:13). The Talmud reads their 'running and returning' (רָצוֹא וָשׁוֹב) as כְּאוֹר הַיּוֹצֵא מִפִּי הַכִּבְשָׁן, 'like the light that comes out of the mouth of a furnace', and a baraita there calls them חַיּוֹת אֵשׁ מְמַלְּלוֹת, fire-creatures that speak — silent when the divine word goes forth, speaking when it does not (b. Ḥagigah 13b).", sources: "WLC/OSHB Ezek.xml 1:5, 1:13, 1:20; 10:15, 10:20; b. Ḥagigah 13b (Vilna text); pointed Yotzer Or; Mishneh Torah, Yesodei ha-Torah 2:7" },
  "the-covering-cherub": { context: "Biblical, in a dirge addressed to the king of Tyre (Ezek 28:11-19): 'you were in Eden the garden of God... you were an anointed covering cherub... you walked up and down in the midst of the stones of fire', until violence was found in him and he was cast from the mountain. Whether this preserves a genuine Israelite myth of an expelled heavenly guardian, or is entirely a poetic figure for a proud king, is disputed among scholars and always has been. What is NOT in the Hebrew is Lucifer: that name comes from the Latin of Isaiah 14:12 rendering הֵילֵל בֶּן שָׁחַר, and the fusion of the two passages into a single fall-of-Satan narrative is a later Christian synthesis read back into Ezekiel.", light: "אַבְנֵי אֵשׁ, 'stones of fire', which he walks among and is expelled from (Ezek 28:14, 16), and the covering of precious stones in 28:13. The imagery is of a being set among burning brightness rather than radiant in itself.", sources: "WLC/OSHB Ezek.xml 28:14, 28:16" },
  "ha-satan": { context: "In Job 1-2 and Zechariah 3 the word takes the definite article, ha-satan, which Hebrew does not do to proper names: this is an office in the divine council. 1 Chronicles 21:1 drops the article, the one place the word may be a name, and the parallel at 2 Samuel 24:1 assigns the same incitement to the LORD's anger. Nothing in the Hebrew Bible ties him to the serpent of Eden or to Isaiah 14's morning star; b. Bava Batra 16a has Resh Lakish make him, the evil inclination and the angel of death one.", sources: "WLC/OSHB Job.xml 1:6, 1:12; Zech.xml 3:1-2; Num.xml 22:22; 1Chr.xml 21:1" },
  "samael": { context: "He is not in the Hebrew Bible or the Mishnah, and the printed Bavli names him once, at Sotah 10b, where he removes the tokens Tamar produced and Gabriel restores them. Pirqei de-Rabbi Eliezer 13, written under Arab rule in the eighth or ninth century, is the text that mounts him on the serpent, and chapter 21 makes him the father of Cain. The pairing with Lilith is later still, first in Isaac ha-Kohen's Treatise on the Left Emanation, Castile, about 1265, and from there into the Zohar.", light: "None of his own. In Kabbalah his side is defined by having no light — Cordovero's הַהַנְהָגָה הַחִיצוֹנִית, the external governance, the shells that receive nothing. His only luminous attribute in Pirqei de-Rabbi Eliezer is arithmetic: twelve wings, against the ḥayyot's four and the seraphim's six.", sources: "Pirqei de-Rabbi Eliezer 13 (pointed Sefaria export text, verified verbatim); Cordovero, Derishot be-Inyanei ha-Malʾakhim; Zohar and 3 Enoch (reported); pointed Hebrew corpus (projectbenyehuda) for the סַמָּאֵל variant" },
  "lilith": { context: "One biblical occurrence and it is contested: Isaiah 34:14 lists her among the beasts of ruined Edom, and whether this is a demon or a bird or animal of the waste is unresolved. The Talmud has her twice in passing: 'whoever sleeps alone in a house, Lilith seizes him' (b. Shabbat 151b), and a woman who 'grows her hair long like Lilith' (b. Eruvin 100b). The dense evidence is not literary at all but the Aramaic incantation bowls of Sasanian Babylonia, fifth to seventh century, where liliths are a whole class of spirit to be bound and divorced. The story that makes her famous — that she was Adam's first wife, refused to lie beneath him, pronounced the Name and flew away — comes from the Alphabet of Ben Sira, a satirical or parodic Hebrew text of roughly the eighth to tenth century. It is not an ancient tradition, and treating it as one is the single commonest error made about her.", light: "None, and the darkness is imposed rather than intrinsic: the night association comes from the folk etymology, not from the sources. What is textually hers is the sleeping house, the solitary bed and the ruined place.", sources: "WLC/OSHB Isa.xml 34:14; b. Shabbat 151b, b. Eruvin 100b (Vilna text); Cordovero, Derishot be-Inyanei ha-Malʾakhim; Alphabet of Ben Sira (reported; edition Stern & Mirsky, Rabbinic Fantasies); Aramaic incantation bowl corpus (reported)" },
  "azazel": { context: "Leviticus gives the word four times and never says what it is — only 'one lot for the LORD and one lot for Azazel', which is what pushes readers towards a name. The rabbis read a place: Mishnah Yoma 6:6 has the goat pushed backwards off a cliff, and b. Yoma 67b derives the word from a rugged mountain while recording that the rite atones for the deeds of Uzza and Azael. Naḥmanides on 16:8 makes him Samael and calls the goat a portion, not a sacrifice. In 1 Enoch 8 and 10 the oldest witnesses read Asael.", light: "Marginal, and only in the Enochic development: among the forbidden arts Azazel is said to have taught humanity are metalwork, ornament and eye-paint — the technologies of appearance. In Leviticus itself there is nothing.", sources: "WLC/OSHB Lev.xml 16:8, 16:10, 16:26; pointed Pirqei de-Rabbi Eliezer; 1 Enoch 8-10 (reported)" },
  "ashmedai": { context: "Gittin 68a-b carries the fullest demon narrative in the Bavli: Benaiah takes him at his pit with wineskins and a chain engraved with the Name, and he ascends daily to the heavenly academy before throwing Solomon four hundred parasangs and reigning in his place. He is a student there, not simply an enemy. Tobit's Asmodaios, centuries earlier, has none of that learning: he kills Sarah's seven husbands at 3:8 and is driven into upper Egypt at 8:3. The derivation from Avestan Aēšma-daēva, 'demon of wrath', is usual but the philology is disputed.", sources: "b. Gittin 68a (Vilna text, disacorns/talmud-bavli-text); Tobit 3, 6, 8 (reported)" },
  "dumah": { context: "In the Psalms the word is a common noun for the silence of the grave, and Dumah is elsewhere a man and a place, Genesis 25:14 and Isaiah 21:11. The angel is Amoraic: at Sanhedrin 94a Rabbi Yoḥanan names the one appointed over the spirits Dumah and reads Isaiah 21:11's 'watchman, what of the night' as their question to him. Berakhot 18b has him announce beforehand who is coming, and Shabbat 152b hands righteous and wicked alike over to him, the one with rest and the other without.", light: "None, and pointedly so. His name is the word for the silence into which the dead go down; he is the exact counter-figure to the ḥayyot of fire and the radiance of the Shekhinah.", sources: "WLC/OSHB Ps.xml 94:17, 115:17; Gen.xml 25:14; Isa.xml 21:11; b. Sanhedrin 94a, b. Berakhot 18b, b. Shabbat 152b (Vilna text, disacorns/talmud-bavli-text); Zohar (reported)" },
  "ahura-mazda": { context: "In the Gāthās the name is two separately declined words used in either order, Ahura Mazdā or Mazdā Ahura; it fuses into a single name only later, as Old Persian Auramazdā. Darius's DNa opens by calling him the great god who created this earth, yonder sky, man, and happiness for man, and who made Darius king — yet no Achaemenid royal inscription names Zarathuštra or the Aməša Spəṇtas, so whether those kings were Zoroastrians is still argued. Yašt 1 lists twenty of his names, recited as protection.", light: "His abode is anaγra raocå, 'the beginningless lights' — the highest heaven, above the graded stations of the stars, the moon and the sun, and the destination the Gāthās ask to reach. Fire, Ātar, is called his son and the visible presence of Ahura Mazdā and his aṣ̌a. The popular gloss 'Ahura means light' is folk etymology; ahura is 'lord', and the luminous associations are theological, not lexical.", sources: "Gāthās (Yasna 28–53); Yašt 1; Vīdēvdād 19; Darius, DB and DNa; Enc. Iranica, 'AHURA MAZDĀ'; Boyce, HZ I; Skjærvø, The Spirit of Zoroastrianism." },
  "vohu-manah": { context: "First-named of the six in most listings, and the one who in the Gāthās conducts Zaraθuštra into Ahura Mazdā's presence (Y 43, 44). Later systematisation assigns each Aməša Spəṇta a creation to protect: his is the cattle, gəuš. In the Gāthās vohu manah is as often a state of mind the worshipper is to acquire as a distinct being; the tradition holds both readings without embarrassment, and forcing a choice between 'abstraction' and 'person' is a modern imposition. The eleventh month and second day of the Zoroastrian calendar carry his name.", light: "No independent luminous office. The moral vocabulary attached to him is one of clear thinking rather than radiance; the light imagery within the heptad belongs to Aṣ̌a Vahišta through fire.", sources: "Yasna 43, 44, 47; Enc. Iranica, 'BAHMAN' and 'AMƎŠA SPƎNTA'; Narten, Die Aməša Spəṇtas im Avesta." },
  "asa-vahista": { context: "Aṣ̌a is the Avestan continuation of Indo-Iranian ṛta, the same word as Vedic ṛta and Old Persian arta. The Ašəm Vohū, his own short prayer, is grammatically so open that no rendering of its three lines has won agreement. Yašt 3, the hymn bearing his name, says almost nothing about him: it praises the Airyaman prayer and lists kinds of healing. The neat allotment of one creation to each of the heptad, fire to him, is set out systematically only in Pahlavi works such as the Bundahišn.", light: "The most light-bearing member of the heptad, by way of his creation: fire, Ātar 𐬁𐬙𐬀𐬭 (letters AA · TE · A · RE), described as 'burning and unburning fire', addressed as mazišta yazata, 'greatest yazata', and treated as the visible presence of Ahura Mazdā and his aṣ̌a. This is the link that makes the fire of the living fire-temples a matter of truth rather than of heat.", sources: "Ašəm Vohū; Yašt 3; Yasna 43, 47; Enc. Iranica, 'AŠA' and 'ĀTAŠ'; Boyce, Zoroastrians." },
  "xsara-vairiia": { context: "'The dominion worth choosing': the rule a person elects within themselves now and that will be fully realised at the end of the world. His creation is metal, which links him to the molten-metal ordeal of the final judgement in Middle Persian eschatology. The sixth month and fourth day of the Zoroastrian calendar are his.", light: "No direct luminous office in the Avesta. The Middle Persian Bundahišn's picture of the sky as a shell of shining metal or crystal is a later cosmological elaboration under his patronage, not an Avestan statement, and should not be cited as one.", sources: "Yasna 51; Enc. Iranica, 'AMƎŠA SPƎNTA'; Bundahišn (for the later metal/sky association); Narten (1982)." },
  "spnta-armaiti": { context: "The one member of the six with an unmistakably feminine identity and the most fully personified — daughter of Ahura Mazdā, and so bound up with the ground that tilling and caring for the earth is service rendered to her. The twelfth month (Esfand) bears her name, and the fifth day of that month is still kept in Iran as Espandagān/Sepandārmazgān, a women's festival — a live observance, not an antiquarian note.", light: "No luminous office; her element is earth. Do not import the Gnostic Sophia here — the occasional modern pairing of Ārmaiti with Sophia is an analogy invented by comparativists, not a link the sources make.", sources: "Yasna 44, 47; Enc. Iranica, 'SPANDARMAD'; Boyce, HZ I; Narten (1982)." },
  "hauruuatat": { context: "Never really appears alone: he and Amərətāt are named as a pair from the Gāthās onward — Y 34.11, 'both Wholeness and Immortality are for sustenance' — and together they are the reward held out to the faithful. Formally an abstract noun in -tāt-, the same suffix as Latin -tās, so the name is closer to 'wholeness-ness' than to a personal name. Third month and sixth day of the calendar.", light: "None. Water, not light.", sources: "Yasna 34.11, 45, 47; Enc. Iranica, 'HORDĀD' and 'AMƎŠA SPƎNTA'; Narten (1982)." },
  "amrtat": { context: "In the Gāthās she is barely separable from Hauruuatāt: the two are named as a pair, and Yasna 47.1 is the one Old Avestan stanza that brings the whole heptad into a single verse. Her charge of plants, and Hauruuatāt's of the waters, is only implicit in the Avesta and is set out systematically first in the Pahlavi books. Her name survives in ordinary use as Mordād, the fifth Iranian month; Lagarde's derivation of the Qurʾān's Hārūt and Mārūt from the pair is much repeated but unproven.", light: "None directly. In later tradition the pair Hauruuatāt/Amərətāt underlies the water and plant offerings of the Yasna rather than any luminous imagery.", sources: "Yasna 34.11, 45, 47; Enc. Iranica, 'AMURDĀD'; Narten (1982)." },
  "spnta-mainiiu": { context: "Yasna 30.3 sets the two mainiiu side by side as twins, though both the word rendered 'twins' and the phrase usually translated 'in a dream' are contested. The reading that makes the Gāthās a monotheism with two subordinate spirits, Ahura Mazdā not himself one of the pair, was argued by Martin Haug in his Essays of 1862 and adopted by Parsi reformers then under missionary attack. Zurvanite theology, which made Ohrmazd and Ahriman the twin sons of Zurwān, is known almost wholly from hostile Armenian and Syriac reports.", light: "spəṇta is not a light-word: 'bounteous, furthering, strengthening' is the sense. The Middle Persian Spenāg Mēnōg is ranged with the luminous mēnōg (spiritual) world against Ahriman's darkness, but that light-versus-dark spatial framing is Sasanian and must not be read back into the Gāthās.", sources: "Yasna 30.3–5, 45.2, 47; Haug, Essays on the Sacred Language, Writings and Religion of the Parsis (1862); Enc. Iranica, 'AHRIMAN' and 'GATHAS'; Kellens & Pirart, Les textes vieil-avestiques." },
  "mira": { context: "The common noun miθra means 'contract, mutual obligation', from mi- 'to bind' with the instrument-suffix -tra-; the god is the personified contract, and to break faith is to be a miθrō.druj, a 'lie-to-Miθra'. Yašt 10, the Mihr Yašt, is among the longest and finest of the Yašts: he has a thousand ears and ten thousand eyes and never sleeps. With Sraoṣ̌a and Rašnu he judges the soul at the Bridge. The seventh month and sixteenth day are his, and Mehregān is still celebrated.", light: "In the Avesta he is emphatically not the sun. Yt 10.13 has him rise over Mount Harā ahead of the immortal swift-horsed sun, the first to reach the light — a herald of dawn, not the disc. His later identification with the sun is a Hellenistic and Middle Iranian development, fossilised in the New Persian noun mehr 'sun'. This makes him a precise case study in how a light-association accrues to a god who did not start with one.", sources: "Yašt 10; Yasna 1.11, 3.13; Enc. Iranica, 'MITHRA'; Gershevitch, The Avestan Hymn to Mithra (1959); Boyce, HZ I–II." },
  "sraosa": { context: "An abstract noun from sru- 'to hear' — 'listening', hence 'obedience' and, in later usage, 'conscience'. One of very few yazatas already named in the Gāthās. Yasna 57 is the Srōš Yašt within the Yasna liturgy and Yašt 11 is also his; he alone among the yazatas has a place in the daily Srōš Bāj, and he beats back the demon Aēšma (Wrath) each night. In living Parsi and Irani practice the three days of prayer after a death are addressed largely to him — this is a name in current devotional use, not a museum piece.", light: "Not a light-figure; his sphere is hearing rather than seeing, and the night is his watch. Any account that makes him an 'angel of light' is importing Abrahamic angelology.", sources: "Yasna 56–57; Yašt 11; Vīdēvdād 18; Enc. Iranica, 'SRAOŠA'; Kreyenbroek, Sraoša in the Zoroastrian Tradition (1985)." },
  "arduui-sura-anahita": { context: "Yašt 5 runs to a formula — who sacrificed, what was asked, whether it was granted — and at 5.17–19 Ahura Mazdā himself sacrifices to her, asking that Zarathuštra be brought to his religion. The stanzas describing her beaver-skin cloak, golden shoes and star-set crown read as a cult image, and are dated to the reign of Artaxerxes II, in whose Susa inscription she is first named beside Auramazdā and Mithra, and to whom Berossos, quoted by Clement of Alexandria, credits her first statues.", light: "Waters rather than light, though the Ābān Yašt calls her radiant. Her later gloss as the planet Venus follows from the Ištar identification, not from anything the Avestan text says, and should not be cited as Avestan.", sources: "Yašt 5 (Ābān Yašt); Enc. Iranica, 'ANĀHĪD i. Ardwīsūr Anāhīd'; Boyce, HZ II; Berossus in Clement of Alexandria (for the Artaxerxes II statues)." },
  "vrrana": { context: "Yašt 14 opens with ten bodies in which he comes to Zarathuštra: wind, gold-horned bull, white horse, rutting camel, boar, youth of fifteen, the vārəγna raptor, ram, wild buck and armed man; a feather of that bird, drawn over the body, is a charm against wounds. Benveniste and Renou argued in 1934 that vərəθra- means obstacle rather than a dragon's name, so that he smashes resistance and is not the Iranian Indra — Indra is in the Avesta twice, at Vīdēvdād 10.9 and 19.43, among the daēuuas.", light: "The youth of fifteen is described as shining-faced and the raptor form in terms of glare and speed, but he is not a light-divinity as such. He is tightly bound to xᵛarənah, the Glory, which the Zamyād Yašt has fleeing in the shape of exactly such a raptor.", sources: "Yašt 14 (Bahrām Yašt); Enc. Iranica, 'BAHRĀM' and 'INDRA'; Benveniste & Renou, Vṛtra et Vṛθragna (1934)." },
  "xvarnah": { context: "Yašt 19 is nominally the hymn to Zam, the earth, and its first eight stanzas list some forty mountains before the Glory is reached. Its central episode has the Glory leave Yima three times in the shape of a vārəγna bird, taken in turn by Miθra, Θraētaona and Kərəsāspa; 19.34 gives the cause of the first departure as Yima's taking delight in false speech. The old claim that Old Persian farnah- is a Median loan has been challenged by Skjærvø and should not be relied on.", light: "The most explicitly luminous concept in the whole Iranian corpus, and the one that makes the Iranian equation of legitimacy with light: the rightful king shines because the Glory rests on him, and its withdrawal is a darkening. Note that the etymology is disputed — the traditional derivation from *hvar 'to shine' (giving 'radiance') is opposed by H. W. Bailey, who derived it from a root 'to obtain', giving 'good things obtained, fortune'. Most Iranists still favour the 'shine' connection, but the light-meaning should be presented as probable, not proven.", sources: "Yašt 19 (Zamyād Yašt); Enc. Iranica, 'FARR(AH)' (Gnoli); Bailey, Zoroastrian Problems in the Ninth-Century Books (1943); Lubotsky, 'Avestan xvarənah-: the etymology and concept', in Sprache und Kultur der Indogermanen, ed. W. Meid (Innsbruck 1998), 479–488; Skjærvø, 'Farnah-: mot mède en vieux-perse?' (BSL 1983) and 'Kayānian Glory'." },
  "ara-mainiiu": { context: "The name stands complete only once in Old Avestan, at Yasna 45.2, and there as description rather than title, where the more bounteous spirit tells the hostile one that nothing of theirs — thought, word, deed, soul — agrees. Yasna 30 has only 'the better and the bad' of two mainiiu. Vīdēvdād 1 answers each of Ahura Mazdā's sixteen lands with an affliction, Airyanəm Vaējah with the serpent in the river and winter. Bundahišn 1 sets Ohrmazd on high in the light and him at the abysmal station in darkness.", light: "His realm is darkness in the Middle Persian cosmology — Ohrmazd on high in endless light, Ahriman below in the endless dark, the void between. That spatial light/dark scheme is Sasanian (Bundahišn) and reading it back into the Gāthās, where the contrast is between mentalities and choices, is a straightforward error.", sources: "Yasna 30, 45; Vīdēvdād 1, 19; Bundahišn 1; Enc. Iranica, 'AHRIMAN'; Skjærvø, The Spirit of Zoroastrianism." },
  "the-fravashis": { context: "The word does not occur in the Gāthās at all; Yašt 13, at 158 stanzas the longest, is the source, and much of it is a roll-call of named men and women whose frauuaṣ̌is are worshipped. Yt 13.80 worships the frauuaṣ̌i of Ahura Mazdā himself, and 13.149 sets the frauuaṣ̌i beside ahu, daēnā, baoδah and uruuan as one of five parts of a person. At 13.49–52 they come back to their houses at Hamaspaθmaēdaya asking to be named and fed — the days still kept as Frawardīgān.", light: "They are said to uphold the sky and to guide the sun, moon and stars along their courses (Yt 13.57) — which places them at the machinery of the lights rather than among the lights themselves. This is one of the clearest places where the Avesta makes the motion of the luminaries a moral and personal act rather than a mechanism.", sources: "Yašt 13 (Frawardīn Yašt); Enc. Iranica, 'FRAVAŠI' and 'FRAWARDĪN YAŠT'; Boyce, HZ I; Malandra, An Introduction to Ancient Iranian Religion." },
  "zurwan": { context: "The earliest notice of an Iranian time-god is in Eudemus of Rhodes (4th c. BCE) as reported much later by Damascius; the developed myth is known chiefly from hostile Armenian, Syriac and Islamic descriptions of Sasanian religion, since no Zurvanite scripture survives. R. C. Zaehner's Zurvan: A Zoroastrian Dilemma (1955) reconstructed a full Zurvanite church and heresy, and that reconstruction has since been substantially dismantled: Shaul Shaked and Albert de Jong argue Zurvanism was never a discrete sect with its own priesthood and institutions but a cosmogonic variant tolerated within Zoroastrian diversity, and de Jong observes that Damascius neither names Zurvan nor reports the twin birth.", light: "None in any Avestan text, and none in the Middle Persian reports either. Modern esoteric and Traditionalist writing that makes Zurvan a primordial light, an Iranian Ain Soph or a pleromatic first principle has no ancient basis whatever and should be labelled as modern if it is used at all.", sources: "Vīdēvdād 19.9 (zruuan- akarana-); Eudemus of Rhodes in Damascius, De principiis; Enc. Iranica, 'ZURVAN' and 'ZURVANISM' (de Jong); Zaehner, Zurvan (1955); Shaked, Dualism in Transformation (1994)." },
  "seraphim": { context: "Isaiah 6 is the only place in the Hebrew Bible where seraphim stand above the throne; elsewhere the word names the fiery serpents of Numbers 21:6-8 and the flying saraph of Isaiah 14:29. Isaiah 6:2 gives them six wings, and in 6:6 one takes the coal from the altar, but no rank is assigned. The word never appears in the New Testament, and their standing as first of nine is Dionysius's own: Celestial Hierarchy 6.2 sets the order, and 7.1 reads the name as burning and heat-making.", light: "The most luminous of all created things in this system: they touch the θεαρχικὴ ἔλλαμψις (the divine-source illumination) without mediation and hand it down. Dionysius's whole angelology is a photagogy — light-giving (φωτοδοσία), rays (αὐγαί), illumination (ἔλλαμψις) are its working vocabulary, and the Seraphim are the first burning of that light.", sources: "Ps.-Dionysius, De caelesti hierarchia 6.2, 7.1 (Migne PG 3, verified in the Calfa–GREgORI Patrologia Graeca corpus); Isaiah 6:2, 6:6 LXX (Rahlfs, verified via the Marcion LXX database); critical ed. Heil–Ritter, Corpus Dionysiacum II." },
  "cherubim": { context: "In the Hebrew Bible the cherubim guard Eden and bear the throne; Dionysius makes them second of nine. At CH 7.1 he glosses the Hebrew name as πλῆθος γνώσεως (‘fullness of knowledge’) or χύσις σοφίας (‘outpouring of wisdom’). They are the knowing rank as the Seraphim are the burning rank.", light: "Their name is glossed as an outpouring — they receive the first light and let it flow. In the Byzantine liturgy the congregation sings the Cherubic Hymn as those who ‘mystically represent the Cherubim’, which is a living, not antiquarian, use of the term.", sources: "Ps.-Dionysius, De caelesti hierarchia 7.1 (PG 3); Genesis 3:24, Ezekiel 10:1 LXX; Exodus 25:18–22." },
  "thrones": { context: "Colossians 1:16 is the only New Testament passage where thronoi name a class of beings rather than seats, and it gives them no description and no rank. Testament of Levi 3:8 places thrones among the ranks where praise is offered without ceasing, and 2 Enoch 20:1 sets many-eyed thrones beside the cherubim and seraphim, though the Testaments survive in Christian recension and 2 Enoch only in Slavonic manuscripts, neither securely older than the letter. The exaltation above all earthly lowness, and the unwavering bearing of God, is Celestial Hierarchy 7.1.", light: "The steady vessel of the thearchic ray: what the Seraphim burn with and the Cherubim know, the Thrones simply and immovably hold.", sources: "Colossians 1:16 (SBLGNT, MorphGNT edition); Ps.-Dionysius, De caelesti hierarchia 6.2, 7.1 (PG 3)." },
  "dominions": { context: "From Colossians 1:16 and Ephesians 1:21. Dionysius takes the name to mean a lordship (κυριότης) that has escaped every unlikeness to true lordship — no grovelling beneath, no tyranny above. They open the second triad, which mediates between the God-facing first triad and the world-facing third.", light: "They receive the illumination of the first triad and lord it over nothing except their own likeness to the source of light; their rule is a transmission, not a possession.", sources: "Colossians 1:16, Ephesians 1:21 (SBLGNT); Ps.-Dionysius, De caelesti hierarchia 8.1 (PG 3)." },
  "powers": { context: "The dynameis appear only inside lists: Ephesians 1:21 sets Christ above every rule, authority, power and dominion, and Romans 8:38 numbers them among the things that cannot separate believers from the love of God. Neither passage ranks them nor calls them good, and the reading of the name as a courageous and unflinching virility is Celestial Hierarchy 8.1's. English usage is treacherous: the Vulgate gives virtutes for dynameis and potestates for exousiai, so in the common English nine the dynameis are the Virtues and Powers names another rank.", light: "Their virtue is the strength not to flinch at the light — they hold the incoming ἔλλαμψις without wavering and pass it on.", sources: "Ephesians 1:21 (SBLGNT); Ps.-Dionysius, De caelesti hierarchia 8 (PG 3, chapter heading ‘περὶ κυριοτήτων, καὶ δυνάμεων, καὶ ἐξουσιῶν’)." },
  "authorities": { context: "Colossians 1:16 lists exousiai among the things created in Christ. The same vocabulary is hostile elsewhere: Colossians 2:15 has Christ disarming the archai and exousiai, Ephesians 6:12 sets the struggle against them, and 1 Corinthians 15:24 has every rule and authority abolished. Celestial Hierarchy 8.1 reads the name instead as a beautiful and unconfused good order that never uses its authority imperiously, a construction the letters themselves do not require.", light: "They order the reception of light into a governable form, so that the third triad can carry it out to the world.", sources: "Colossians 1:16 (SBLGNT); Ps.-Dionysius, De caelesti hierarchia 8 (PG 3; critical ed. Heil-Ritter, Corpus Dionysiacum II); Scholia on the Celestial Hierarchy, printed under Maximus's name in PG 4, much of it assigned to John of Scythopolis by Suchla's edition." },
  "principalities": { context: "The archai are named at Colossians 1:16, and again at Ephesians 6:12 among the hostile rulers; the New Testament does not settle their standing. Celestial Hierarchy 9.2 gives the nations not to them but to the Angels, naming Michael ruler of the Jewish people and quoting the Septuagint of Deuteronomy 32:8, where the boundaries of the nations follow the number of the angels of God. Gregory the Great's thirty-fourth homily on the Gospels ranks them differently within the nine, and Aquinas records both arrangements in the Summa Theologiae.", light: "The point at which the light begins to be directed outward, toward the hierarchies that human beings can enter.", sources: "Colossians 1:16 (SBLGNT); Ps.-Dionysius, De caelesti hierarchia 9 (PG 3)." },
  "archangels": { context: "The word archangelos occurs twice in the New Testament, at 1 Thessalonians 4:16 and Jude 9, and only Jude gives it a name, Michael. Gabriel in Luke 1:19 and Raphael in Tobit 12:15 say only that they stand in God's presence, Raphael as one of seven, and neither text calls them archangels. The seven are unfixed: 1 Enoch 20 names six in the Ethiopic and seven in the Greek of the Panopolitanus manuscript, which adds Remiel. Celestial Hierarchy 9.2 puts them in one order with the Principalities and Angels.", light: "A hinge in the transmission: they receive from above in a more hidden way and give below in a more manifest one.", sources: "1 Thessalonians 4:16, Jude 9 (SBLGNT); Luke 1:19; Tobit 12:15 (Rahlfs) for the seven who stand before the glory; Ps.-Dionysius, De caelesti hierarchia 9 (PG 3; Heil-Ritter, Corpus Dionysiacum II); 1 Enoch 20, six archangels in the Ethiopic, Remiel added in the Greek Panopolitanus text." },
  "angels": { context: "Celestial Hierarchy 5 explains why the lowest rank lends its name to all nine: the higher orders hold the illuminations and powers of the lower, while the lowest have no share in what stands above them, so the theologians may call the Seraphim angels but never the angels seraphim. The corpus itself is late, dated to about 500 on its dependence on Proclus, first cited by Severus of Antioch between about 518 and 528, and rejected as apostolic by Hypatius of Ephesus at the Constantinople colloquy of 532.", light: "The last stage of the descent of light, and the one at which it reaches a human hierarchy — Dionysius's ἐκφαντορικά, the ‘manifesters’ of the thearchic illumination.", sources: "Ps.-Dionysius, De caelesti hierarchia 5 and 9 (PG 3; critical ed. Heil-Ritter, Corpus Dionysiacum II); Scholia on the Celestial Hierarchy in PG 4, ed. Suchla; for the dating and reception, Koch and Stiglmayr (1895) on the dependence on Proclus, the citations in Severus of Antioch, and the Constantinople colloquy of 532." },
  "christ-the-logos": { context: "John uses Logos as a title only at 1:1 and 1:14 and never again in the Gospel; it returns at Revelation 19:13 and, as the word of life, at 1 John 1:1. John 1:9 is contested in its grammar, since coming into the world can attach to the light or to everyone it enlightens. Gnostic texts place the names differently: Irenaeus pairs Logos with Zoe among the Valentinian aeons of the Ogdoad at Against Heresies 1.1.1, while the Apocryphon of John gives the title Christ to the anointed Autogenes.", light: "The controlling image of the whole tradition: ‘the true light that enlightens everyone’ (John 1:9), the light Dionysius calls the θεαρχικὴ ἀκτίς whose descent through the ranks is the celestial hierarchy, and the light whose theft and recovery is the Gnostic plot.", sources: "John 1:1, 1:9, 1:14 (SBLGNT, MorphGNT edition); Revelation 19:13; 1 John 1:1; Irenaeus, Adv. haer. 1.1.1 for Logos and Zoe in the Valentinian Ogdoad, 1.29.1-2 for the Barbeloite Autogenes-Christ (Greek fragment, First1KGreek); Nag Hammadi Coptic corpus (Ap. John NHC II,1 with the BG 8502 parallel; Gospel of Truth NHC I,3)." },
  "sophia": { context: "“Sophia” covers more than one figure. Irenaeus, Adversus haereses 1.1–1.2, reports the Ptolemaean scheme in which she is the youngest of the thirty aeons, checked by Horos, her Enthymesis and its passion cut away to become the Achamoth of 1.4; at 1.29.4 and 1.30, which survive in the Latin, she is instead Prunicus. The Apocryphon of John has her act without her consort and “without the consent of the Spirit”, and afterwards sets her not in her own aeon but “in the ninth until she has corrected her deficiency”.", light: "Her substance is light: what she loses to Yaldabaoth is a portion of light-power (ⲇⲩⲛⲁⲙⲓⲥ), and the entire cosmic drama is its recovery. In Ap. John she is renewed by the Epinoia of light (ⲉⲡⲓⲛⲟⲓⲁ, 40× in the corpus) sent into Adam.", sources: "Irenaeus, Adv. haer. 1.2 (Greek preserved among the Valentinians in Epiphanius, Pan. 31; First1KGreek), 1.29.4 and 1.30.2–3 (cited from the Latin version); Hippolytus, Refutatio 6.30–31; Ap. John NHC II,1 and III,1; Hypostasis of the Archons NHC II,4; On the Origin of the World NHC II,5." },
  "barbelo": { context: "The Apocryphon of John has the invisible Spirit behold himself in the light-water surrounding him, and his Thought stand forth: “This is the first thought, his image; she became the womb of everything.” She is then active, requesting foreknowledge, indestructibility, eternal life and truth in turn, each granted as an aeon. Her titles there run Mother-Father, first man, thrice-male, and thrice-named androgynous one. The derivation of the name is unresolved; Coptic, Hebrew and numerical proposals all circulate and none has carried the field.", light: "She is described in light-language throughout: she comes to be when the Spirit beholds himself in luminous water, and she is ‘the perfect glory’, the first light in which the unknowable becomes visible to itself.", sources: "Ap. John NHC II,1 pp. 4–5 (Coptic verified against the Marcion Nag Hammadi database; standard ed. Waldstein & Wisse, The Apocryphon of John: Synopsis of NHC II,1; III,1; IV,1 with BG 8502,2, NHMS 33, Brill 1995); Irenaeus, Adv. haer. 1.29.1 (cited from the Latin version); Epiphanius, Pan. 25.2, 26.1." },
  "yaldabaoth": { context: "In the Apocryphon of John he is Sophia’s offspring, “a lion-faced serpent” whose “eyes were like lightning fires”; he took a great power from his mother and withdrew, ignorant, “thinking that there existed no other except his mother alone”. He bears three names there — Yaltabaoth, Saklas, Samael — and begets twelve authorities, seven set over the seven heavens and five over the depth of the abyss, so the neat seven planetary archons is a compression. Origen’s Contra Celsum preserves from Celsus the Ophite ascent-formula addressed to him by name.", light: "He is the anti-luminary: a being of fire and darkness who possesses stolen light he cannot see by. In Ap. John he is expressly ‘ignorant’, and the whole redemptive action consists in getting light past him.", sources: "Ap. John NHC II,1 (ⲓ̈ⲁⲗⲧⲁⲃⲁⲱⲑ) and NHC III,1 (ⲓ̈ⲁⲗⲇⲁⲃⲁⲱⲑ); Hypostasis of the Archons NHC II,4; On the Origin of the World NHC II,5; Origen, Contra Celsum 6.31–32 (Greek, First1KGreek/Perseus); Epiphanius, Pan. 25–26; Irenaeus, Adv. haer. 1.30." },
  "autogenes": { context: "The Apocryphon of John has the invisible Spirit place “the divine Autogenes of truth over everything” and subject every authority to him; the four lights come forth from Autogenes and receive their occupants — Pigera-Adamas by Armozel (Harmozel elsewhere), Seth by Oriel (Oroiael), the seed of Seth by Daveithai, the souls who repented late by Eleleth. In Zostrianos and Allogenes he is rearranged: not a son beside Barbelo but the lowest of three levels within her aeon, below Kalyptos and Protophanes.", light: "He is enthroned over the four φωστῆρες, the Luminaries Harmozel, Oroiael, Daveithai and Eleleth, and stands with them in the light of the invisible Spirit — the structural centre of the Sethian light-world.", sources: "Ap. John NHC II,1 and III,1; Gospel of the Egyptians NHC III,2 and IV,2; Zostrianos NHC VIII,1; Three Steles of Seth NHC VII,5; Irenaeus, Adv. haer. 1.29.2 (Greek fragment, First1KGreek)." },
  "achamoth": { context: "Strictly Valentinian. The upper Sophia is restored to the Pleroma; her abortive offspring, Achamoth, remains outside, and is shaped ‘according to knowledge’ by the Saviour who comes to her with his angels. From her converted passions come the Demiurge and the world-stuff. Irenaeus's Greek (Adv. haer. 1.4.5) shows her looking on the Saviour and taking power from his appearing. The Coptic Gospel of Philip's distinction between ⲉⲭⲁⲙⲱⲑ and ⲉⲭⲙⲱⲑ shows the name in use inside the school, not merely in its opponents.", light: "She turns to the light that left her and is formed by it; the light she cannot keep becomes the psychic substance of the cosmos. Her passion is described as being outside the light and reaching back toward it.", sources: "Irenaeus, Adv. haer. 1.4.5–1.5.6 (Greek fragments preserved via Epiphanius, Pan. 31; First1KGreek); Hippolytus, Refutatio 6.30–34; Gospel of Philip NHC II,3 60.10–15; First Apocalypse of James NHC V,3; Hebrew: Proverbs 9:1 (OSHB / Westminster Leningrad Codex)." },
  "the-demiurge": { context: "The word is Plato’s: at Timaeus 28a the dēmiourgos frames the cosmos on an eternal model, and no censure attaches. Irenaeus, Adversus haereses 1.5, gives the Valentinian version — Achamoth’s offspring, maker of seven heavens, seated in the Hebdomad beneath her Ogdoad, saying in ignorance “I am God, and beside me there is none other”. Ptolemy’s Letter to Flora, at Epiphanius, Panarion 33, has him just and in the middle, neither the perfect God nor the devil; it is the Sethian and Ophite texts that make the maker hostile.", light: "He works from an image of a light he has not seen. In the Tripartite Tractate the Logos acts through the Demiurge, who does not know that a higher one exists.", sources: "Tripartite Tractate NHC I,5; A Valentinian Exposition NHC XI,2; Irenaeus, Adv. haer. 1.5; Hippolytus, Refutatio 6.32–34; Plato, Timaeus 28a for the term's origin." },
  "the-ogdoad-and-the-hebdomad": { context: "In the Valentinian system Achamoth dwells in the Ogdoad and the Demiurge below her in the Hebdomad; in the Sethian texts the Hebdomad is Yaldabaoth's seven planetary rulers, ‘the sevenness of the week’. The Hermetic Discourse on the Eighth and Ninth (NHC VI,6) uses the same vocabulary for an ascent — ⲑⲟⲅⲇⲟⲁⲥ ⲙⲛ̄ ⲑⲉⲛⲛⲁⲥ, ‘the Ogdoad and the Ennead’ — showing the terms crossing school boundaries. The Ogdoad also names the first eight aeons of the Valentinian Pleroma, a THIRD and distinct use.", light: "The ladder image is astronomical: seven planetary spheres of darkening rule, then the eighth of fixed light, and the soul's ascent is a passage upward through them, shedding at each what that ruler gave it.", sources: "Ap. John NHC II,1 and III,1; Gospel of the Egyptians NHC III,2 and IV,2; Discourse on the Eighth and Ninth NHC VI,6; A Valentinian Exposition NHC XI,2; Irenaeus, Adv. haer. 1.5.2–3; Hippolytus, Refutatio 6.31–34; Origen, Contra Celsum 6.31." },
  "the-father-of-greatness": { context: "Mani’s own books survive only in fragments, so this figure is known mainly from Theodore bar Konai’s Syriac Book of Scholia, written about 792 by a hostile Christian, from the fourth-century Coptic Kephalaia, and from the Turfan fragments. Syriac calls him abbā d-rabbūtā; Middle Persian either renders that literally as pid ī wuzurgīh or substitutes the Zoroastrian god-name Zurwān, a usage belonging to the Iranian mission. Chapter 21 of the Kephalaia is the locus for his fivefold structure.", light: "He IS the Realm of Light in person — its king, its five limbs, and the origin of every particle of light later scattered through the cosmos and progressively recovered.", sources: "W. Sundermann, ‘Manicheism ii. The Manichean Pantheon’, Encyclopaedia Iranica; Theodore bar Konai, Liber Scholiorum XI (Syriac); Coptic Kephalaia; P. O. Skjærvø, An Introduction to Manicheism. Language forms here rest on secondary report, not on my own inspection of an edition." },
  "the-living-spirit": { context: "Theodore bar Konai’s Book of Scholia, written about 792, is the fullest witness. There the Living Spirit — Syriac rūḥā ḥayyā — calls down to the defeated Primal Man, and the Call and the Answer themselves become gods; the fabric he raises is counted as ten heavens and eight earths. His five sons are Splenditenens, the Great King of Honour, Adamas of Light, the King of Glory and the Porter. Unlike the Sethian chief archon he builds deliberately, by design.", light: "He is the engineer of light: sun and moon are the two luminous ships he builds to ferry rescued light-particles upward, and the whole visible sky is his refinery.", sources: "Theodore bar Konai, Liber Scholiorum XI; W. Sundermann, ‘Manicheism ii. The Manichean Pantheon’, Encyclopaedia Iranica; Coptic Kephalaia and Psalm-Book. Language forms rest on secondary report." },
  "the-column-of-glory": { context: "Syriac esṭūn šubḥā, Latin columna gloriae, Parthian bāmistūn; the Coptic material identifies it with the Perfect Man, so it is a person as much as a road. Its equation with the Milky Way is the standard modern reading, given in Sundermann’s Encyclopaedia Iranica articles, and is an inference from the system rather than a surviving line of Mani’s own. The Syriac noun carries praise as well as glory, whence the alternative rendering Column of Praise.", light: "Wholly a being of light and literally visible: the Milky Way as the ladder of ascending souls, with the moon filling and emptying as the ferry that receives them.", sources: "W. Sundermann, ‘Manicheism ii. The Manichean Pantheon’ and ‘Cosmogony and Cosmology iii. In Manicheism’, Encyclopaedia Iranica; Coptic Kephalaia. Language forms rest on secondary report. (Welburn, Mani, the Angel and the Column of Glory, 1998, is a translated anthology with interpretive commentary from a non-academic press: not to be cited for language forms or for the Milky Way identification.)" },
  "indra": { context: "Roughly a quarter of the Ṛgveda is addressed to him, more than to any other god: 2.12.7 counts among his deeds that he begot the sun and the dawn, and 2.12.3 that he drove the cows out from Vala's hiding-place. The same hymn does not assume assent — at 2.12.5 men ask where he is, and some say outright that he does not exist. The Purāṇas keep him and demote him, a king who loses, most familiarly to Kṛṣṇa at Govardhana in the Bhāgavata Purāṇa.", light: "Not a light-god as such. His element is the storm and the vajra, the lightning-flash; but the Ṛgveda credits him with winning the sun and the dawns and 'finding the light' (svar) by splitting Vala, so light is his spoil rather than his substance.", sources: "Amarakośa 1.1.51, 1.3.3 (GRETIL-derived e-text); Ṛgveda 2.12.3, 2.12.5, 2.12.7" },
  "agni": { context: "Agnim īḷe purohitam, the first line of the Ṛgveda, is addressed to him, and some two hundred hymns follow. The Aitareya Brāhmaṇa begins by calling him avama, lowest of the gods, with Viṣṇu highest and all the others between the two. Bṛhadāraṇyaka Upaniṣad 4.3.6 then sets him inside a descending series of lights: when sun, moon, fire and speech have all gone out, a man's light is the self.", light: "Central, and the most useful being here for a register about light. शोचिष्केश means 'flame-haired'; कृष्णवर्त्मन् 'black-tracked' names the smoke behind the flame, so his light is defined against its own residue. In Upaniṣadic thought the fire's light becomes a standing image for the light of consciousness.", sources: "Amarakośa 1.1.64–65; Ṛgveda 1.1.1; Hiraṇyakeśi Gṛhyasūtra and Bodhāyana Dharmasūtra (pratīkas)" },
  "surya": { context: "Ṛgveda 1.115.1 names him the eye of Mitra, of Varuṇa and of Agni — three, where the usual formula keeps only two — and calls him the ātman of what moves and what stands still. The team is not fixed: 1.50.8 yokes seven bay mares to him, while elsewhere a single steed, Etaśa, draws him. The Puruṣasūkta at 10.90.13 reverses the relation and has the sun born from the primal man's eye.", light: "Absolutely central — he is the luminary itself. Keep him lexically distinct from ज्योतिस् jyotis (light in general), तेजस् tejas (fiery potency), प्रकाश prakāśa (the act of making manifest) and प्रभा prabhā (emitted lustre). Sanskrit maintains these distinctions carefully and collapsing them loses the tradition's actual optics.", sources: "Ṛgveda 1.115.1, 1.50.8, 7.63.2, 10.90.13; Amarakośa 1.1 (Āditya synonyms) — the Halāyudha reference as printed, 'Abhidhānaratnamālā 1.1.1.35–37', is a four-level string that does not fit a text cited by kāṇḍa and verse, and a synonym lexicon cannot support the entry's claims in any case" },
  "savitr": { context: "Ṛgveda 3.62.10, the Sāvitrī verse imparted at upanayana, asks for the bhargas of the god, his radiance — may we take it to ourselves, and may he impel our thoughts. The separation from Sūrya is sharper in the commentators than in the hymns: Sāyaṇa holds that the sun is Savitṛ before rising and Sūrya from rising to setting, while 5.81.4–5 has Savitṛ himself become Mitra by his ordinances and Pūṣan by his journeyings. He is hiraṇyapāṇi at 1.35.9 and hiraṇyahasta, golden-handed, at 1.35.10.", light: "The activity of solar light rather than its body. Savitṛ is golden-handed and golden-tongued and his work is impulsion; the Vedic poets maintain a real distinction between the sun as luminary (Sūrya) and the sun as impeller (Savitṛ), which is worth preserving rather than harmonising.", sources: "Ṛgveda 3.62.10, 1.35.9–10, 5.81.4–5; Hiraṇyakeśi Gṛhyasūtra (Gāyatrī at upanayana) — the Halāyudha reference as printed, 'Abhidhānaratnamālā 1.1.1.35', does not resolve against that text's kāṇḍa-and-verse numbering" },
  "usas": { context: "About twenty hymns are hers, the largest body of goddess-poetry in the Ṛgveda. At 7.77.3 she carries the eye of the gods and leads in the white horse, and the verse ends uṣā adarśi raśmibhir vyaktā, 'Dawn has been seen, made manifest by her rays'. The measuring has a hard edge: 1.124.2 has her keep the divine ordinances undiminished while wearing down the generations of men. The corpus also turns on her — at 4.30.8–11 Indra smashes her wagon and she flees from it.", light: "Among the most light-saturated beings in the entire corpus. RV 7.77.3: उषा अदर्शि रश्मिभिर् व्यक्ता — 'Dawn has appeared, made manifest by her rays'; RV 7.77.5 asks her to shine forth with her best beams. She is the daily re-arrival of visibility as such, and the Ṛgvedic hymns to her are the tradition's primary poetry of light.", sources: "Ṛgveda 6.64.6, 7.77.3–6 (GRETIL Devanagari e-text); Pāṇini, Aṣṭādhyāyī 4.2.31, 6.3.31" },
  "varuna": { context: "The Vasiṣṭha hymns, Ṛgveda 7.86–89, are the tradition's confession literature: the poet asks what his offence was, and 7.86.6 answers that it was not his own will but wine, anger, dice and thoughtlessness. What the texts give him is surveillance rather than radiance — spaśaḥ, spies who go about among men, and Atharvaveda 4.16, in which the winkings of men's eyes are counted. The pairing of his night against Mitra's day is a Brāhmaṇa formula, not a Ṛgvedic one.", light: "Not a light-deity, but the counterpart of one. Where Mitra holds the day, Varuṇa is associated with the night sky and with the stars as his thousand watching eyes; his omniscience is figured as seeing rather than as shining.", sources: "Amarakośa 1.3.3; Bodhāyana Dharmasūtra and Hiraṇyakeśi Gṛhyasūtra (RV 1.25 pratīka); Ṛgveda 7.86–89; Atharvaveda 4.16 — the 'Aitareya Brāhmaṇa 1.4 (Varuṇa with the Ādityas)' reference should go: that khaṇḍa is the pravargya and the reassembling of the sacrifice, and nothing in the entry depends on it" },
  "visnu": { context: "Only some five or six Ṛgvedic hymns are his, yet 1.22.20 already reaches the tradition's highest note: the patrons forever behold that supreme step of Viṣṇu, spread out like an eye in heaven. The Aitareya Brāhmaṇa opens by ranking him highest of the gods and Agni lowest, with all the others between the two — a standing his handful of hymns does not by itself explain. The dark blue-black colouring and the Kaustubha jewel are epic and Purāṇic; the Saṃhitā has neither.", light: "Solar in origin — the strides measure the sun's course — but later iconography makes him dark blue-black (śyāma) rather than radiant, with the Kaustubha jewel and the discus carrying the luminous charge instead.", sources: "Amarakośa 1.1.21; Śaiva Siddhānta ritual MSS (विष्णवे नमः, viśeṣāntyeṣṭi I00048)" },
  "siva": { context: "In the Ṛgveda śiva is an adjective, 'auspicious', used even of Agni and Indra; the god is Rudra, and the name is first heaped on him litanically in the Śatarudrīya at Taittirīya Saṃhitā 4.5.8, namaḥ śivāya ca śivatarāya ca. The prakāśa and vimarśa pair is not Śaiva Siddhānta's: it is the non-dualist Pratyabhijñā of Utpaladeva and Abhinavagupta, while Siddhānta is dualist and does not build on it. The third eye that burns Kāma belongs to the Purāṇas and to Kālidāsa, though tryambaka stands already at Ṛgveda 7.59.12.", light: "The fire of the third eye and the cool crescent of चन्द्रशेखर, 'moon-crested', held together in one figure. In Śaiva Siddhānta and Pratyabhijñā his deeper light is प्रकाश prakāśa, the self-luminosity of consciousness, paired with विमर्श vimarśa, its self-reflection — light as awareness rather than as illumination of objects.", sources: "Amarakośa 1.1.36–37; Śaiva Siddhānta ritual manuscripts (Muktabodha collection)" },
  "devi": { context: "In the Ṛgveda devī is an epithet and not a title: 6.64.6 calls Uṣas uṣo devi, and Rātrī, Sarasvatī and Pṛthivī take it too. A goddess already speaks in her own voice at 10.125, where Vāc says she carries Mitra and Varuṇa; but the text that first gathers the goddesses into one supreme Goddess is the Devīmāhātmya, chapters 81 to 93 of the Mārkaṇḍeya Purāṇa, near the sixth century. Umā Haimavatī stands between, appearing at the close of Kena Upaniṣad 3 to tell Indra that the yakṣa was brahman.", light: "RV 6.64.6 and 7.77.5 address Uṣas as उषो देवि, the earliest surviving use of देवी for a luminous goddess. In later Śākta metaphysics the Goddess is चित्शक्ति cit-śakti, the light and power of consciousness itself.", sources: "Amarakośa 1.1.45–47; Ṛgveda 6.64.6, 7.77.5 (GRETIL Devanagari)" },
  "durga": { context: "Attested by name in Amarakośa 1.1.46, अपर्णा पार्वती दुर्गा मृडानी चण्डिकाम्बिका — that is, the lexicon lists her among the Goddess's names, not as an independent deity. Her defining text is the Devīmāhātmya, where the gods, unable to defeat Mahiṣa themselves, pool their power to produce her. Mahiṣāsuramardinī imagery from the Kuṣāṇa period indicates a pre-Purāṇic history. Durgā Pūjā remains among the largest living festivals in eastern India.", light: "In the Devīmāhātmya she is constituted from the massed तेजस् tejas — fiery radiance — of the assembled gods. This is one of the tradition's most explicit statements that a divine body can be made of light, and is directly usable in a register about luminous beings.", sources: "Amarakośa 1.1.46; Devīmāhātmya (Mārkaṇḍeya Purāṇa) — cited from the standard text, not fetched here" },
  "kali": { context: "Named in Amarakośa 1.1.45 among the Goddess's names (उमा कात्यायनी गौरी काली हैमवतीश्वरी). In the Devīmāhātmya she springs from Durgā's brow in wrath; in the Kālikā Purāṇa and the Śākta tantras she becomes supreme, above all in Bengal, Assam and Kashmir. Her name is the feminine of काल, which means both 'black' and 'time' — both readings are traditional and the texts themselves exploit the pun rather than choosing between them.", light: "A goddess of darkness rather than light: कālī is 'the black one', night-coloured in iconography. But Śākta and Krama theology treat that blackness as the ground out of which illumination arises and into which it returns, not as mere privation — a genuinely different account of darkness from the Western privative one, and worth stating carefully rather than assimilating.", sources: "Amarakośa 1.1.45; Devīmāhātmya; Kālikā Purāṇa — the latter two cited from standard texts, not fetched here" },
  "ganesa": { context: "Ṛgveda 2.23.1 does call its addressee gaṇapati, but the hymn is to Brahmaṇaspati and names him in the same line, so it is not evidence for this god. The oldest vināyakas are the four seizing spirits of the Mānava Gṛhyasūtra, to be got rid of rather than petitioned, whom the Yājñavalkya Smṛti collapses into one Vināyaka appointed by Rudra and Brahmā. The elephant-headed form is firmly attested only from about the fifth century.", light: "Little connection with light; his attributes are the broken tusk, noose and goad, and the modaka. Any luminous role assigned to him in modern correspondence-tables is imported, not traditional.", sources: "Amarakośa 1.1.47; Ṛgveda 2.23.1 and Hiraṇyakeśi Gṛhyasūtra (for the gaṇapati verse — see cautions)" },
  "brahma": { context: "The Ṛgveda has the neuter brahman and the priest brahmán but no god of this name; Brahmā the creator emerges in late Vedic and epic material, largely absorbing Prajāpati, and never gains a temple cult on the scale of Viṣṇu's or Śiva's. His tie to earth is ritual and late: the Śaiva Viśeṣāntyeṣṭi manual gives him the yellow square earth-maṇḍala as its kāraṇeśvara, and Pūrṇānanda's Ṣaṭcakranirūpaṇa seats him in the mūlādhāra with the śakti Ḍākinī, where the Śiva Saṃhitā names no Brahmā at all.", light: "Not a light-deity in his own right. His placement at the base of the subtle body, in the densest element, is the opposite pole from the luminous ākāśa at the throat.", sources: "Śaiva Siddhānta ritual MSS (viśeṣāntyeṣṭi I00048, ब्रह्मणे नमः); Ṣaṭcakranirūpaṇa / Śiva Saṃhitā tradition" },
  "sadasiva": { context: "The five faces are named in the pañcabrahma mantras of the Taittirīya Āraṇyaka — Sadyojāta, Vāmadeva, Aghora, Tatpuruṣa, Īśāna — but those mantras neither present a five-faced deity of that name nor give the faces colours; both are Āgamic. The prakāśa and vimarśa pair is the non-dualist Pratyabhijñā's rather than dualist Śaiva Siddhānta's, and there it belongs first to Paramaśiva; what marks the Sadāśiva tattva is the faint 'I am this' where an object first appears. Pūrṇānanda's Ṣaṭcakranirūpaṇa sets him at the throat as Ardhanārīśvara, with the śakti Śākinī.", light: "In Śaiva metaphysics the Sadāśiva level is where प्रकाश prakāśa (the light of consciousness) and विमर्श vimarśa (its self-recognition) stand in balance — the point at which light becomes self-aware rather than merely illuminating. This is the most philosophically loaded 'light' claim in the whole Indian group.", sources: "Śaiva Siddhānta ritual MSS: samayadīkṣādhivāsanavidhi M00564, viśeṣāntyeṣṭi I00048, parārthapūjā I00058 (Muktabodha digitisations); Taittirīya Āraṇyaka for the Pañcabrahma mantras" },
  "isana": { context: "The mantra he takes his name from, in the Taittirīya Āraṇyaka, calls him lord of all knowledges and ruler of all beings and closes on the word sadāśiva — which is where his name and Sadāśiva's meet, and why the two are so easily run together. That mantra gives him neither quarter nor colour: the crystal comes from Āgamic ritual, where the Viśeṣāntyeṣṭi has him govern an ether-maṇḍala like pure crystal, with Sadāśiva as its cause-lord. Pūrṇānanda's Ṣaṭcakranirūpaṇa seats Īśa elsewhere again, at the heart, with the śakti Kākinī.", light: "The north-east is the auspicious quarter, and in Śaiva dhyāna the Īśāna face is described as crystal-white — the most transparent and least coloured of the five faces, corresponding to the subtlest of the elements it governs.", sources: "Amarakośa 1.1.36–37; Śaiva Siddhānta MSS (viśeṣāntyeṣṭi I00048, parārthapūjā I00058, vāmādimūrdhānta installation sequence)" },
  "dakini": { context: "The word is post-Vedic. The Bhāgavata, Brahma and Mārkaṇḍeya Purāṇas and the Kathāsaritsāgara place ḍākinīs among the flesh-eating attendants of Kālī; the Veda and the epics have nothing of the kind. The cakra-śakti meant here belongs to a much later systematisation: the Śiva Saṃhitā seats her in the base lotus as first of six, before Rākiṇī, Lākinī, Kākinī, Śākinī and Hākinī, and Pūrṇānanda's sixteenth-century Ṣaṭcakranirūpaṇa gives her four arms. Buddhist tantra uses the same word for an awakened female being — one name over two offices.", light: "Not a light-being. Her associations are the earth element, the base of the body, and the colour red in the standard dhyāna verses.", sources: "Śiva Saṃhitā (mūlādhāra passage); Ṣaḍāmnāyapūjāvidhi M00556 and Ūrdhvāmnāyapūjāvidhi M00422 (Nepalese Sarvāmnāya MSS, Muktabodha)" },
  "sakyamuni": { context: "84000: \"An epithet for the historical Buddha, Siddhārtha Gautama: he was a muni ('sage') from the Śākya clan. He is counted as the fourth of the first four buddhas of the present Good Eon, the other three being Krakucchanda, Kanakamuni, and Kāśyapa. He will be followed by Maitreya.\" Note that Śākyamuni is an epithet, not a personal name — the personal and clan name is Siddhārtha Gautama (सिद्धार्थ गौतम). Dates are unsettled: CC-CEDICT still repeats the older 563–485 BCE, while current scholarship generally places the death substantially later, around 400 BCE.", light: "Not a light-being. His awakening is figured with dawn imagery and he is called \"eye of the world\", but radiance as an identity belongs to Amitābha and Vairocana, not to him.", sources: "84000 glossary entity-43916, entity-28430; CC-CEDICT 釋迦牟尼, 釋迦牟尼佛, 釋迦佛" },
  "amitabha": { context: "84000: \"The buddha of the western buddhafield of Sukhāvatī, where fortunate beings are reborn to make further progress toward spiritual maturity. Amitābha made his great vows to create such a realm when he was a bodhisattva called Dharmākara. In the Pure Land Buddhist tradition, popular in East Asia, aspiring to be reborn in his buddha realm is the main emphasis.\" 84000 treats Amitābha (\"Infinite Light\") and Amitāyus (\"Infinite Life\") as names of one buddha and files them as synonyms; Tibetan and East Asian practice often keep them apart as distinct forms with distinct functions, Amitāyus being invoked for longevity. Sukhāvatī rebirth is an aspiration, not a heaven in the theistic sense: it is a realm where practice is easier, not a final destination.", light: "Central, and etymologically so. The name is amita-ābha, \"measureless light\", and the Tibetan འོད་དཔག་མེད། translates it exactly — 'od \"light\", dpag med \"immeasurable\". 84000 renders one entry \"Amitābha, 'Infinite Light'\" and CC-CEDICT glosses 阿彌陀如來 \"Buddha of infinite light\". Of the whole Buddhist pantheon he is the being whose name simply is light.", sources: "84000 glossary entity-35445, entity-19375, entity-36712 (Amitāyus synonymy); CC-CEDICT 阿彌陀佛, 阿彌陀如來, 彌陀" },
  "vairocana": { context: "Sanskrit vairocana is a vṛddhi form of virocana, 'the sun', and means 'belonging to the sun'; the East Asian name Dainichi, 'Great Sun', renders the expanded Mahāvairocana. His scriptural base is the Mahāvairocana-abhisaṃbodhi Tantra, put into Chinese by Śubhakarasiṃha and Yixing in 724-725. The centrality is class-specific rather than general: the Yoga tantras, the Sarvatathāgatatattvasaṃgraha above all, set him at the centre, while the Guhyasamāja moves him east and seats Akṣobhyavajra in the middle.", light: "His identity. Vairocana derives from virocana, \"shining, illuminating\"; 84000 translates the name \"The Illuminator\" and \"Completely Illuminating\", and one entry as \"Brilliance of the Sun\". The Tibetan རྣམ་པར་སྣང་མཛད། means \"the one who makes fully luminous/manifest\", and the Chinese 大日 is literally \"Great Sun\". If any Buddhist figure belongs in an architecture of light, it is this one.", sources: "84000 glossary entity-35931, entity-26947, entity-31633, entity-22645; CC-CEDICT 大日如來" },
  "maitreya": { context: "84000 files him flatly as \"Maitreya (Skt: maitreya): A buddha\", while the Śākyamuni entry places him in the future: \"He will be followed by Maitreya, the next buddha in this eon.\" Both are correct in their own frame, and the double classification is a good test of whether a register is reading tense carefully. 84000 lists maitra, maitrī and the synonym Ajita under the same Tibetan headword; the Tibetan བྱམས་པ། byams pa translates the root maitrī, \"loving-kindness\", so the Tibetan name means \"the Loving One\" rather than transliterating the Sanskrit. The fat, laughing 彌勒 of Chinese temples (笑口彌勒) is not the Indian Maitreya at all but the 10th-century Chinese monk Budai, identified with him by later Chinese tradition.", light: "None.", sources: "84000 glossary entity-34667, entity-19059, entity-41981, entity-14429; CC-CEDICT 彌勒, 彌勒佛, 彌勒菩薩" },
  "avalokitesvara": { context: "84000: \"One of the 'eight close sons of the Buddha,' he is also known as the bodhisattva who embodies compassion. In certain tantras, he is also the lord of the three families, where he embodies the compassion of the buddhas. In Tibet, he attained great significance as a special protector of Tibet, and in China, in female form, as Guanyin, the most important bodhisattva in all of East Asia.\" Three figures are routinely collapsed and should not be: the Indian bodhisattva, male; Tibetan Chenrezig, patron of Tibet, of whom the Dalai Lamas are held to be emanations — a Tibetan claim in its developed form, not a pan-Buddhist one; and Chinese Guanyin, represented as female from roughly the Song dynasty onward. The etymology is genuinely disputed among scholars: the older Chinese 觀世音 (\"perceiver of the world's sounds\") appears to translate a form Avalokitasvara (अवलोकितस्वर, \"sound perceived\"), attested in some Sanskrit manuscripts, whereas the Tibetan long form and the later Chinese Guānzìzài render Avalokita-īśvara, \"the lord who looks down\". Which is prior is unresolved.", light: "Indirect but real: his office is seeing. Tibetan སྤྱན་རས་གཟིགས། is literally \"looking with the eye\", and the Chinese 觀 guān is \"to contemplate, perceive\" — this is a being defined by an act of attention turned toward suffering rather than by radiance.", sources: "84000 glossary entity-41544; CC-CEDICT 觀音, 觀世音, 觀世音菩薩, 普陀山" },
  "manjusri": { context: "84000: \"Mañjuśrī is one of the 'eight close sons of the Buddha' and a bodhisattva who embodies wisdom. He is a major figure in the Mahāyāna sūtras, appearing often as an interlocutor.\" He carries the sword that cuts through confusion and the Prajñāpāramitā volume. The Tibetan epithet \"sole father of the buddhas\" — because he provokes their realisation of the profound — and the sung form Mañjughoṣa (Tib. འཇམ་དཔལ་དབྱངས། 'jam dpal dbyangs, \"Jamyang\") are Tibetan usages. In China he is lord of Mount Wutai, one of the four sacred mountains. The name means roughly \"gentle glory\", not anything to do with light.", light: "None in the name. Wisdom is conventionally figured as light dispelling darkness, and his sword is sometimes shown flaming, but treating Mañjuśrī as a light-being would be an imposition.", sources: "84000 glossary entity-42550, entity-2946; CC-CEDICT 文殊, 文殊菩薩, 文殊師利菩薩" },
  "ksitigarbha": { context: "84000 records him as \"One of the celestial bodhisattvas ... A bodhisattva mahāsattva ... One of the sixteen great bodhisattvas,\" noting that the membership of that list varies from text to text. CC-CEDICT: \"the Bodhisattva of the Great Vow (to save all souls before accepting Bodhi); also translated Earth Treasury, Earth Womb, or Earth Store Bodhisattva\" — the name is kṣiti \"earth\" + garbha \"womb, matrix, store\", and the Tibetan སའི་སྙིང་པོ། sa'i snying po (\"heart/essence of the earth\") translates it. He is included here precisely because his weight is regionally lopsided: the cult of Dizang in China and Jizō in Japan is enormous, and rests largely on the Kṣitigarbha Sūtra, whose Indian origin is widely doubted by scholars, who take it for a Chinese composition. In Tibetan practice he is canonically present but marginal.", light: "None.", sources: "84000 glossary entity-31031, entity-14409; CC-CEDICT 地藏, 地藏菩薩, 地藏王菩薩, 大願地藏菩薩" },
  "samantabhadra": { context: "This is the clearest case in the whole register of a Tibetan claim that must not be read back as pan-Buddhist. In the Mahāyāna sūtras and throughout East Asia, Samantabhadra — Chinese 普賢 Pǔxián, \"Universal Worthy\", whom CC-CEDICT glosses \"the Buddhist Lord of Truth\" — is a bodhisattva, the companion of Mañjuśrī, famous for the ten great vows that close the Gaṇḍavyūha, and lord of Mount Emei. In the Nyingma school, Kuntuzangpo is a different order of thing entirely: the primordial dharmakāya buddha who was never deluded, depicted naked and deep blue, in union with Samantabhadrī, and the source of the Dzogchen transmission. 84000's canonical entries show yet another usage, where samantabhadra is a personal name or a place name. Treating all of these as one figure is a category error.", light: "In the Nyingma sense, decisively: Kuntuzangpo personifies the ground's own self-luminous awareness (rig pa), the light that was never lit because it was never out. In the Mahāyāna bodhisattva sense, none.", sources: "84000 glossary entity-20986, entity-2993, entity-26865; CC-CEDICT 普賢菩薩" },
  "tara": { context: "84000 declines to fix her class, and that hedging is the finding: \"A goddess whose name can be translated as 'Savior.' She is known for giving protection and is variously presented in Buddhist literature as a great bodhisattva or a fully awakened buddha,\" and elsewhere \"the chief goddess of the activity family, personifying the true nature of the element wind; one of the five goddesses personifying the five 'hooks of gnosis'.\" Tārā enters Indian Buddhism around the 6th century and is absent from the early canon. The Green/White pair, the twenty-one Tārās, and her role as the national protectress alongside Chenrezig are Tibetan and Newar systematisations that should not be projected onto Indian sources.", light: "White Tārā carries moonlight and longevity associations in Tibetan practice. But the etymology usually offered — tārā as \"star\" — is a homonym, not the derivation the tradition uses: the Tibetan translation སྒྲོལ་མ། sgrol ma (\"she who delivers across\") assumes the root √tṝ, \"to cross over\", which Rangjung Yeshe spells out as \"She who Takes (beings) Across (the Ocean of Samsara)\". The star reading is a widespread later association and should be flagged as such.", sources: "84000 glossary entity-36756; Rangjung Yeshe s.v. sgrol ma" },
  "vajrapani": { context: "84000 lays the career out explicitly: \"Vajrapāṇi means 'Wielder of the Vajra.' In the Pali canon, he appears as a yakṣa guardian in the retinue of the Buddha. In the Mahāyāna scriptures he is a bodhisattva and one of the 'eight close sons of the Buddha.' In the tantras, he is also regarded as an important Buddhist deity and instrumental in the transmission of tantric scriptures.\" That is an unusually well-documented case of a being changing class as the literature changes, and it is a standing warning against flattening: which Vajrapāṇi you mean depends on which corpus you are reading. 84000 also notes that in one text Vajradhara (वज्रधर, རྡོ་རྗེ་འཆང་། rdo rje 'chang, glossary/2594) is \"another name for Vajrapāṇi\" — but in Tibetan Vajrayāna, Vajradhara is a distinct primordial buddha, so that synonymy is corpus-specific and must not be generalised.", light: "None.", sources: "84000 glossary entity-43961, entity-2594; CC-CEDICT 金剛手菩薩, 金剛" },
  "yamantaka": { context: "84000: \"Yamāntaka (Skt: yamāntaka): The wrathful aspect of Mañjuśrī,\" and under the related name Yamāri, \"'Yama's Enemy,' an epithet of Yamāntaka, the wrathful form of Mañjuśrī.\" The logic is precise and worth preserving: because Yama is a deva and therefore himself inside saṃsāra and subject to karma, he can be ended — and it is wisdom (Mañjuśrī) in wrathful form that ends him. The Vajrabhairava form is the principal yidam of the Geluk school and a specifically Tibetan development; it should never be presented as pan-Buddhist. Wrathfulness here is directed at death and ignorance, not at beings: a wrathful deity is not a demon, and the register should not let the iconography suggest otherwise.", light: "None.", sources: "84000 glossary entity-2360, entity-42478 (Yamāri)" },
  "vajrayogini": { context: "84000 records her with deliberate brevity — \"Vajrayoginī (Skt: vajrayoginī): A Buddhist goddess\" — and separately Vajravārāhī, \"A Buddhist goddess related to Vajrayoginī with the face of a sow.\" She belongs wholly to the higher tantras and to Tibetan and Newar practice; she has no place in Theravāda and essentially none in mainstream East Asian Buddhism, so any register that lists her as simply \"Buddhist\" is overreaching. She is included here to make the ḍākinī class concrete rather than abstract. RESPECT: her practice is transmitted only under empowerment, and practitioners treat her full iconography, sādhana and mantra as material not to be reproduced casually or displayed for decoration.", light: "None, though she is standardly described as red and blazing.", sources: "84000 glossary entity-2344, entity-8859 (Vajravārāhī)" },
  "yama": { context: "The Devadūta-sutta, Majjhima Nikāya 130, has him question the wrongdoer about the five messengers he ignored, then fall silent while the niraya-pālas, not he, carry out the punishment. The same sutta gives him a wish of his own: to gain the human state, meet a Tathāgata and understand the Dhamma — the aspiration of a being still within saṃsāra. The Aṅguttara parallel at 3.36 counts three messengers, and commentarial tradition makes him a vemānika-peta with four Yamas at the four gates.", light: "None; his quarter is the south and his domain is below.", sources: "84000 glossary entity-41622, entity-42427, entity-36023; CC-CEDICT 閻羅, 閻羅王, 閻王, 閻魔" },
  "mahakala": { context: "84000 is unusually careful here and its caution should be carried over verbatim in substance: \"Mahākāla is a wrathful Buddhist protector deity. In Tibetan, the name Mahākāla was mostly translated literally with nag po chen po ('Great Black One') but on occasion it was rendered mgon po nag po ('Black Lord') ... Outside the Buddhist tradition, Mahākāla is also a name for a wrathful form of Śiva.\" And explicitly: \"Not to be confused with the protectors in the later higher tantras ... or with Śiva who also has this name (though then it has the alternative meaning of 'Great Time').\" Three things share this name — the Śaiva Mahākāla, the sūtra-level yakṣa, and the Six-Armed Mahākāla of Tibetan higher tantra — and identifying them is exactly the sort of late syncretism read back into early texts that a careful register must flag. Rangjung Yeshe's \"wrathful aspect of Avalokiteshvara\" is a Tibetan doctrinal position, not a statement about the Indian sources. RESPECT: Mahākāla practice is empowerment-restricted in Tibetan traditions.", light: "Inverse, and punningly so. kāla means both \"black\" and \"time\", and 84000 flags precisely that ambiguity: the Buddhist name is read as \"Great Black\", the Śaiva as \"Great Time\". He is the one figure here defined by darkness.", sources: "84000 glossary entity-2643, entity-43292, entity-35534; Rangjung Yeshe s.v. nag po chen po" },
  "mara": { context: "Māra is the one who assailed Śākyamuni beneath the Bodhi tree. 84000 records the tangle of names around him: Antaka, \"Alternate name of Māra\"; and Namuci — \"Originally the name of Indra's principal enemy among the asuras. In early Buddhism he appears as a drought-causing demon and eventually his name becomes that of Māra, the principal opponent of the Buddhadharma.\" That transfer of a name from an asura to Māra is a real textual development, not a modern conflation. Crucially, the tradition also uses \"the four māras\" as an analytical list of obstacles — the aggregates, the afflictions, death, and the devaputra-māra — so māra is simultaneously a person and a category, and Tibetan བདུད། bdud carries both. Reading Māra as \"the Buddhist Satan\" imports a dualism Buddhism does not hold: he is a long-lived, powerful being inside saṃsāra, neither eternal nor the counter-pole of a good god.", light: "None.", sources: "84000 glossary entity-43007, entity-35329 (Antaka), entity-43881 (Namuci)" },
  "palden-lhamo": { context: "This entry exists to mark a boundary rather than to fill a slot. The Sanskrit Śrī of the sūtras 84000 translates — \"The great goddess Śrī, better known as Lakṣmī, who promises to aid those who recite this sūtra and to ensure its preservation\" — is an Indian goddess of fortune, and she is not the same figure as the Tibetan Palden Lhamo: the wrathful dark-blue protectress riding a mule across a sea of blood, who carries local and very probably pre-Buddhist Tibetan material. Rangjung Yeshe's equations (\"mahakali Skt. shri devi\", \"A female Mahakala\") are Tibetan identifications made after the fact. Presenting Palden Lhamo as \"the Buddhist Śrī\" would be exactly the error of reading a late Tibetan synthesis back into an Indian text. RESPECT: she is an empowerment-restricted protector and the object of living monastic and, historically, state ritual.", light: "None.", sources: "84000 glossary s.v. dpal ldan lha mo (Śrī / Lakṣmī entry); Rangjung Yeshe s.v. dpal ldan lha mo" },
  "yuanshi-tianzun-celestial-worthy-of-primordial-beginning": { context: "The presiding deity of the Lingbao 靈寶 corpus and first of the Three Pure Ones. In the Duren jing he is the one who 說經 'speaks the scripture' — the text is framed as his revelation, delivered ten times over to the assembled heavens. He is not a creator who makes the world but the point at which the primordial qi becomes articulate as text; the scripture, not the world, is what he produces. Firmly canonical and early: attested densely in the Duren jing (Six Dynasties, c. 400 CE) and throughout the Yunji qiqian.", light: "Substantial. The Duren jing's promise to the adept is 不經苦惱，身有光明，三界侍衞，五帝司迎 — 'he shall not pass through suffering; his body will have radiance (guangming); the Three Realms will attend him as guard; the Five Thearchs will preside over his welcome'. Radiance here is the acquired condition of the transformed body, not an attribute of the deity's appearance. He is seated above the Three Pure Realms in the Daluo Heaven 大羅天.", sources: "Yuanshi wuliang duren shangpin miaojing 元始無量度人上品妙經 (the Duren jing), Kanripo KR5a0001; Yunji qiqian 雲笈七籤 (Zhang Junfang, c. 1029), Kanripo KR5d0055." },
  "lingbao-tianzun-celestial-worthy-of-numinous-treasure": { context: "This is the entry where careless sources go wrong. The modern standard triad name 靈寶天尊 is demonstrably late — it is absent from the Duren jing and from the great Song anthology Yunji qiqian, where the figure is 太上道君. The Yunji qiqian's own structural account gives instead the Three Treasure Lords: 靈寶君治在上清境，即禹餘天也 'Lingbao jun governs in the Shangqing Realm, that is, the Yuyu Heaven', and he 說十二部經為洞玄教主 'speaks the twelve-part scriptures as teaching-master of the Dongxuan [division]'. The triad's real logic is bibliographic: each Lord personifies one of the 三洞 sāndòng, the three divisions of the Daoist canon.", light: "Chiefly through the name. 靈 líng ('numinous') is glossed in the Shuowen as 巫以玉事神 'the wu-shaman serves the spirits with jade', and the variant graph is written with 巫. His realm, the Yuyu Heaven, belongs to the Shangqing 上清 'Highest Clarity' order — the 清 qīng of all three realms means clear, limpid, translucent, so the Three Purities are named for clarity rather than for brightness as such.", sources: "Yunji qiqian KR5d0055 (Three Treasure Lords and Three Caverns); Duren jing KR5a0001; Shuowen jiezi KR1j0018 for 靈. Negative evidence for 靈寶天尊 from exhaustive grep of the sampled corpus." },
  "daode-tianzun-celestial-worthy-of-the-way-and-its-virtue": { context: "Laozi 老子, the putative author of the Daode jing, was deified from the Han onward — the imperial cult to him is second-century CE — and as 太上老君 he became the god who repeatedly descends to instruct sage rulers. He is the one member of the triad with a human biography behind him, which is why he is the most anthropomorphic and the most narratively active of the three. In the Three Treasure Lords scheme his structural counterpart is 神寶君 Shenbao jun, who 治在太清境，即大赤天也 'governs in the Taiqing Realm, that is, the Dachi Heaven', and is 洞神教主 'teaching-master of the Dongshen division'.", light: "Little that is distinctive. His realm 大赤天 'Great Red Heaven' is named by colour rather than luminosity, and his cult is textual and pedagogic rather than photic.", sources: "Yunji qiqian KR5d0055; Duren jing KR5a0001 (太上老君於太丹…); Daozang title 太上老君開天經 cited in Yunji qiqian." },
  "taiyi-the-great-one": { context: "The single most important pre-Daoist high god, and the pivot on which the whole question of the Wuxing correlates turns. The Shiji's Treatise on the Feng and Shan sacrifices states flatly 天神貴者太一，太一佐曰五帝 — 'the most honoured of the celestial spirits is Taiyi; Taiyi's assistants are called the Five Thearchs'. The Han altar complex embodied this: 五帝壇環居其下 'the altars of the Five Thearchs ringed it below'. In the Chuci's Nine Songs he heads the sequence as 東皇太一 'Eastern August Taiyi', the commentators noting that each title in the cycle names a god of Chu.", light: "Direct and astral. The commentaries identify him with the pole: 北辰北極有五星，太乙常居中，是極星也 'the Northern Chronogram, the North Pole, has five stars; Taiyi constantly dwells at the centre — this is the Pole Star', and 其一明者，太乙常居也 'the bright one: this is where Taiyi constantly dwells'. The weft text Wenyao gou adds 中宮大帝，其精北極星 'the Great Thearch of the Central Palace: his essence is the Pole Star'. He is the still bright point the sky turns around.", sources: "Shiji 史記 Fengshan shu, Kanripo KR2a0001; Chuci zhangju 楚辭章句 (Wang Yi), Kanripo KR4a0002; Liji zhushu 禮記注疏 KR1d0053, quoting Chunqiu wei Wenyao gou 春秋緯文耀鉤 and Chunqiu Hecheng tu 春秋合誠圖." },
  "taiyi-jiuku-tianzun-celestial-worthy-who-saves-from-suffering": { context: "The Daoist salvific deity par excellence, and the clearest case of Buddhist form absorbed into a Daoist body: his function, his vow to answer those who call his name, and his iconography (enthroned on a nine-headed lion) run closely parallel to the bodhisattva Guanyin, and scholars generally read the parallel as deliberate adaptation rather than coincidence. Yet the name he is given is the oldest Chinese high-god name there is, 太一. The Yunji qiqian records an image of him being struck by two monks — evidence of a live cult and of inter-religious friction. Canonical, with an entire Daozang class of 救苦 texts.", light: "Indirect: he descends into the dark of the earth-prisons 酆都 to bring souls out. His later honorific 青玄上帝 pairs 青 (blue-green) with 玄 (dark), the colours of the deep sky rather than of radiance.", sources: "Duren jing KR5a0001; Yunji qiqian KR5d0055; Daozang titles 太上救苦天尊說消愆滅罪經 (KR5b0062), 太一救苦天尊說拔度血湖寶懺 (KR5b0241)." },
  "the-jade-emperor-yuhuang-dadi": { context: "Here is the commonest single error about this pantheon: the Jade Emperor is NOT the head of the Daoist canonical pantheon. The Three Pure Ones outrank him. He is the head of the *bureaucracy* — the celestial counterpart of the reigning human emperor, receiving reports and memorials, promoting and demoting gods — which is why he dominates popular religion, temple practice and vernacular fiction while occupying a subordinate rank in the canon's own cosmology. He is comparatively late: an obscure figure before the Tang, raised to prominence by Song imperial patronage (the Zhenzong and Huizong reigns, eleventh to early twelfth century, conferred his grand titles as state policy). His scripture, the Gaoshang Yuhuang benxing jijing, is a Song product.", light: "Explicit and architectural. His scripture places him 玄苑玉皇宮殿昇光明座 'in the Mysterious Garden, at the Jade Sovereign's palace, ascending the Seat of Radiance', in a 大光明殿 'Hall of Great Radiance' furnished with 光明座 'the Seat of Radiance', banners, canopies and rare flowers. He also 分其身徧於十方 'divides his body throughout the ten directions' — radiance as sovereign display and omnipresence.", sources: "Gaoshang Yuhuang benxing jijing 高上玉皇本行集經, Kanripo KR5a0010; CC-CEDICT; the Song court's role is standard in the scholarship on Daoist–imperial relations." },
  "xiwangmu-queen-mother-of-the-west": { context: "The best-documented transformation in the Chinese pantheon, and a warning against reading the late figure back into the early one. The Shanhaijing describes her as 其狀如人，豹尾虎齒而善嘯，蓬髮戴勝 — 'her form is like a human's, with a leopard's tail and tiger's teeth, and she is good at howling; her hair is wild and she wears a sheng headdress' (Guo Pu glossing 勝 as a jade ornament). Her job there is 是司天之厲及五殘 'she presides over the Catastrophes of Heaven and the Five Destructive [Stars]' — a fierce plague- and punishment-power. Only later does she become the beautiful sovereign of Mount Kunlun who receives King Mu at the Jasper Pool 瑤池 and dispenses the peaches of immortality. She also had a genuine mass movement: the Han popular panic of 3 BCE. Scholars dispute how far the archaic and the courtly figures are continuous.", light: "Her mountain is 玉山 'the Jade Mountain', and her 勝 headdress is jade. She belongs to the west and so to 太陰 the Great Yin and the setting sun; the 五殘 she governs are stars, and the Kangxi zidian confirms from the Shiji's astronomical treatise that 五殘星 is a star name. Her radiance is lapidary and lunar rather than solar.", sources: "Shanhaijing 山海經 (Guo Pu ed.), Kanripo KR3l0090; Mu tianzi zhuan material quoted there; Shiji KR2a0001; Kangxi zidian KR1j0048; CC-CEDICT." },
  "zaoshen-the-stove-god": { context: "The god of the domestic religion, and the one most people actually dealt with. His classical root is not a personal god at all but a sacrifice: the Liji's Yueling assigns 其祀竈 'its sacrifice is to the stove' to summer, the Fire phase, and the stove is one of the 五祀 wǔsì, the five household cults. Personification comes later. His decisive later feature is that he holds the office of 司命 — his full temple title is typically 九天東廚司命灶王府君, 'Director of Destinies of the Eastern Kitchen of the Nine Heavens' — so the household's private god is simultaneously a post in the celestial civil service, filing an annual report. The custom of smearing his mouth with malt sugar before he departs is popular religion, not canon.", light: "Fire and hearth: he belongs to the Fire phase in the Yueling scheme, alongside the Red Thearch and Zhurong. His is the domesticated fire, the flame that is watched rather than the flame that shines.", sources: "Liji 禮記 Yueling and Jifa, Kanripo KR1d0052; Shuowen jiezi KR1j0018 (竈 entry, with the variant 𡧌 noted as 竈或不省); CC-CEDICT for modern forms." },
  "leigong-the-duke-of-thunder": { context: "An old popular god whose iconography is documented unusually early — by a sceptic. Wang Chong, arguing in the Lunheng (c. 80 CE) that thunder is a natural phenomenon, describes what the painters of his day drew: 之容，謂之雷公，使之左手引連鼓，右手推椎，若擊之 — 'this figure they call Leigong, showing him with his left hand drawing a linked string of drums and his right hand wielding a mallet, as if striking them'. Wang Chong then demolishes it: 今雷公有形，雷聲有器，安得為神 'now Leigong has a body and thunder has an instrument — how can it be a shen?' and 人時見鬼，有見雷公者乎 'people sometimes see ghosts; has anyone ever seen Leigong?'. So the beaked, drum-ringed thunder god is attested by the first century CE. From the Song he is systematized into the 雷法 'Thunder Rites' and the Thunder Department, a whole bureaucratic sub-pantheon.", light: "Lightning, but strictly as his consort's department: 雷 is the sound, 電 the flash, and the tradition splits them between two persons. Diànmǔ is conventionally shown holding two mirrors that flash the lightning — a light-deity produced by dividing a storm into its noise and its brightness.", sources: "Lunheng 論衡 (Wang Chong), Leixu chapter, Kanripo KR3j0080; Duren jing KR5a0001 for 電母; CC-CEDICT." },
  "the-five-spirits-of-the-seasons": { context: "This answers the Wuxing question directly: YES, the five phases are personified in the sources, and the Liji's Yueling does it in a fixed formula that names two beings per phase, a 帝 and a 神. Extracted complete from the text: spring/Wood 其帝大皞，其神句芒; summer/Fire 其帝炎帝，其神祝融; centre/Earth 其帝黃帝，其神后土; autumn/Metal 其帝少皞，其神蓐收; winter/Water 其帝顓頊，其神玄冥. Note that the 帝 are euhemerized mythic sovereigns while the 神 are functional powers — Zhurong is fire itself, Houtu the earth. The edition varies its own graphs (少暭 twice, 少皞 once); 大皞 is also written 太皞/太昊 and 少皞 as 少昊. These are correlates in a system, not a family of gods, and they were assembled by Warring States and Han systematizers out of older independent figures.", light: "Distributed by phase. 皞 hào, in both Tai Hao and Shao Hao, means 'bright, brilliant'. 祝融 is fire. 玄冥 is 'dark and obscure', the north and winter — the system's dark pole. So light and dark are positions in the scheme rather than properties of individuals.", sources: "Liji 禮記 Yueling 月令, Kanripo KR1d0052; character readings verified in CC-CEDICT (蓐 rù, 皞 hào, 句 gōu, 冥 míng)." },
  "the-five-directional-thearchs-under-their-esoteric-names": { context: "A second, quite separate layer of Wuxing personification, and one that is genuinely esoteric — these are secret cult-names, not the ordinary colour-titles. Their source is named precisely in the commentary: 春秋緯文耀鉤云：蒼帝曰靈威仰，赤帝曰赤熛怒，黃帝曰含樞紐，白帝曰白招拒，黑帝曰汁光紀 — 'the Chunqiu weft-text Wenyao gou says: the Azure Thearch is called Lingweiyang…'. The commentary also gives the ritual calendar: 蒼帝靈威仰，立春之日祭之於東郊 'the Azure Thearch Lingweiyang is sacrificed to on the day of Beginning of Spring, in the eastern suburb', and so through the year. These names come from the 讖緯 chenwei apocrypha of the Han, a body of texts later proscribed, which is why they feel occult. Do not confuse this set with the Yueling's 帝/神 pairs; and note that in the Shiji's Han cult these Five Thearchs are Taiyi's subordinates.", light: "Named into two of them: 汁光紀 contains 光 guāng 'light', and 靈威仰 opens with 靈 líng 'numinous'. Otherwise the light is calendrical — each Thearch is received at the moment its season's light begins.", sources: "Liji zhushu 禮記注疏 (Zheng Xuan's commentary with Kong Yingda's subcommentary), Kanripo KR1d0053, quoting Chunqiu wei Wenyao gou 春秋緯文耀鉤; Shiji KR2a0001 for the Han altar arrangement." },
  "the-seven-star-lords-of-the-northern-dipper": { context: "The Dipper is the single most important structure in Daoist astral religion, and its seven stars are persons with ranks. The Beidou benming yansheng zhenjing enumerates them as 北斗第一 through 北斗第七 and assigns each a cohort of clients by birth-year branch: 貪狼太星君，子生人屬之 'the Star Lord Tanlang the Great: those born in a zi year belong to him'; 巨門元星君，丑亥生人屬之; 祿存貞星君，寅戌; 文曲紐星君，卯酉; 廉貞罡星君，辰申; 武曲紀星君，巳未; 破軍關星君，午. This is the 本命 běnmìng natal-star system, still the basis of living ritual. Two further hidden stars are named, 洞明外輔星君 and 隱光內弼星君. The same seven names — Tanlang, Jumen, Lucun, Wenqu, Lianzhen, Wuqu, Pojun — are the stars of Chinese fate-calculation and Ziwei astrology; the astronomical names of the same seven stars are a different set (天樞, 天璇, 天璣, 天權, 玉衡, 開陽, 搖光).", light: "They are literally stars, and the Yunji qiqian personifies the Dipper's pivot: 璇璣者，北斗君也，天之侯王也 'the Xuanji is the Lord of the Northern Dipper, the marquis-king of heaven'. The two supernumerary stars are named for light and its absence — 洞明 'penetrating brightness' and 隱光 'hidden light' — which is how the tradition accounts for stars said to be there but not seen.", sources: "Taishang xuanling beidou benming yansheng zhenjing 太上玄靈北斗本命延生眞經, Kanripo KR5c0003, with the commentary KR5c0146; Yunji qiqian KR5d0055." },
  "doumu-mother-of-the-dipper": { context: "Demonstrably late, and I can date her from the evidence rather than from assertion: 斗姆 has ZERO occurrences in the Yunji qiqian of 1029 CE, and her scripture, the Taishang xuanling Doumu dasheng yuanjun benming yansheng xinjing, is catalogued as Southern Song (Daozang DZ 621, Dongshen division, Benwen class). She is also the most thoroughly Buddhist-derived major deity in the Daoist pantheon: she is widely identified by scholars with the Tantric goddess Mārīcī (Chinese 摩利支天), from whom she takes her multiple arms and heads and her associations. That identification is standard but the mechanism and date of the transfer are debated, so treat the equation as probable rather than settled.", light: "Strong, and this is where the Mārīcī connection tells, since Mārīcī's name means 'ray of light'. Her own scripture opens 斗姆上靈光圓大天寶月中有騫樹 — 'Doumu, supreme numen, perfect round radiance, great heavenly treasure; within the moon there is a qian tree' — placing her in lunar light, and continues 色瑩瑠璃 'her colour glistening like lapis-glass'. She 降以大藥普垂醫治 'sends down the great medicine and universally dispenses healing'.", sources: "Taishang xuanling Doumu dasheng yuanjun benming yansheng xinjing 太上玄靈斗姆大聖元君本命延生心經, Kanripo KR5c0002 (its own metadata: 正統道藏, DZ0621, 洞神部本文類, dated 南宋?); negative evidence from exhaustive grep of Yunji qiqian KR5d0055; OpenCC for the 斗/鬥 ambiguity." },
  "huangdi-the-yellow-thearch": { context: "'Yellow Emperor' is the received English but 'Yellow Thearch' is more accurate — he is a 帝 in the archaic sense. He occupies three quite different roles that sources often conflate. As a figure of history he opens the Shiji, whose first chapter is the Wudi benji 五帝本紀; the Shiji reports 黃帝者 with a full euhemerized biography including the battle of Zhuolu. As a cosmological correlate he is the Yueling's Thearch of the Centre, 其帝黃帝，其神后土, paired with the Earth phase — and, under the weft-text names, 含樞紐. As a Daoist patriarch he is a master of the arts who finally ascended as a transcendent, and the addressee of the Huangdi neijing, the foundational medical canon. Whether any historical figure lies behind him is not a live scholarly question — he is a construct of Warring States and Han myth-making — but the layering of his roles is genuinely contested.", light: "Positional rather than photic: yellow is the colour of the centre and of earth in the five-phase scheme, so his 'brightness' is the axial brightness of the middle. His Daoist ascent narratives use the standard idiom of rising in daylight.", sources: "Shiji 史記 Wudi benji, Kanripo KR2a0001; Liji Yueling KR1d0052; Baopuzi neipian KR5f0019; Shanhaijing KR3l0090; CC-CEDICT." },
  "siming-director-of-destinies": { context: "This is the entry that answers the office-versus-being question, because 司命 is a job description that at least five different things hold. It is a star name in astronomy. It is one of the household 五祀/七祀 in the Liji, which simply lists 曰司命. It is two personified Chu deities in the Nine Songs, 大司命 the Greater and 少司命 the Lesser. It is a celestial bureau in Daoist texts, running on paperwork — the Taiping jing says 死有期，司命奉籍簿 'death has its term; Siming holds the register-ledger'. And it becomes a title of the Stove God, who reports the household annually. Ge Hong gives the accounting precisely: 大者司命奪紀，小過奪筭，隨所輕重，故所奪有多少也 — 'for grave offences Siming deducts a ji, for lesser faults a suan, according to gravity, so the amount deducted varies' (a 紀 is conventionally 300 days, a 算 three). The lesson: in this pantheon a post can outlast, precede and multiply its holders, and the sources are usually explicit about which they mean. Translating 司命 as a proper name throughout destroys this.", light: "None inherent, though as a star name it belongs to the astral register. Its instrument is the written ledger, not illumination.", sources: "Liji 禮記 KR1d0052; Chuci zhangju KR4a0002; Baopuzi neipian 微旨 KR5f0019; Taiping jing chao 太平經鈔 KR5e0001; Shuowen jiezi KR1j0018; Lunheng KR3j0080." },
  "chenghuang-the-city-god": { context: "The purest example of an office in the Chinese pantheon, and the complement to Siming. The name means simply 'wall and moat' — the city's defences deified. What makes it an office is that its incumbent is a named dead human: the post is filled by the spirit of a deceased worthy local official, and different cities have different men in the same job. Incumbents could be, and were, promoted, demoted and replaced by imperial decree; the Ming founder's edict of 1370 systematically ranked City Gods to mirror the civil administrative hierarchy, so that the celestial and terrestrial bureaucracies were formally isomorphic. This is popular and state religion rather than Daoist canon, though Daoist priests officiate at its temples. Late as a system: Tang beginnings, Song spread, Ming standardization.", light: "None. His attributes are judicial — registers, the abacus, the tablet — and his temples are courtrooms, deliberately dim.", sources: "Yunji qiqian KR5d0055; Duren jing KR5a0001 (社令城隍); CC-CEDICT. The Ming ranking edict is standard in the scholarship on late imperial local cult." },
  "jibril": { context: "Named only three times in the Qurʾān, each time in a defensive or polemical setting. Q 2:97 answers an objection by identifying the bringer of revelation: 'whoever is an enemy to Jibrīl — he it is who brought it down upon your heart by God's leave.' Q 2:98 pairs him with Mīkāl; Q 66:4 ranks him among the Prophet's supporters. He is far more prominent in hadith, where he appears in human form (the ḥadīth Jibrīl, in which he questions the Prophet about islām, īmān and iḥsān) and accompanies the ascension. The name is a Hebrew/Aramaic loan, Gabrîʾēl, 'man' or 'strength of God'.", light: "Not himself called luminous in the Qurʾān, but as an angel he belongs to the order 'created from light'. Q 53:5-11 describes the Prophet seeing him 'on the highest horizon', which the tradition reads as a vision of his true form filling the sky. His title al-Rūḥ al-Amīn ties him to the descent of revelation, figured throughout the tradition as an illumination of the heart.", sources: "Qurʾān 2:97, 2:98, 66:4 (Tanzil Ḥafṣ text + independent Uthmānī cross-check; 3 occurrences, exhaustively verified); Ṣaḥīḥ Muslim; Musnad Aḥmad; EI² art. 'Djabrāʾīl'." },
  "mikal": { context: "A single Qurʾanic occurrence, Q 2:98, where he stands beside Jibrīl among those enmity toward whom makes one an enemy of God. The Qurʾān assigns him no function whatsoever: his portfolio of rizq, rain and vegetation is entirely from tafsīr and hadith. He appears in the well-known supplication 'O God, Lord of Jibrīl, Mīkāʾīl and Isrāfīl' (Ṣaḥīḥ Muslim) and in reports of Badr, where he flanks the Prophet's standard.", light: "No independent light attribution; he shares the angelic substance of light. Exegetes sometimes contrast his portfolio with Jibrīl's as sustenance (water and earth) against revelation (light and knowledge), but this is a homiletic pairing, not a scriptural one.", sources: "Qurʾān 2:98 (sole occurrence, verified in two digital editions); Ṣaḥīḥ Muslim, Ṣalāt al-musāfirīn; Musnad Aḥmad (38 hadith attestations of مِيكَائِيل verified); EI² art. 'Mīkāl'." },
  "israfil": { context: "The Qurʾān never names him: at 39:68 the Trumpet is blown and the blower left unnamed. The name enters through hadith, and its securest attestation is not eschatological at all — Ṣaḥīḥ Muslim 770 has ʿĀʾisha report the Prophet opening his night prayer, 'O God, Lord of Jibrīl and Mīkāʾīl and Isrāfīl'. The Tirmidhī hadith on the Trumpet speaks only of ṣāḥib al-qarn, the owner of the horn; joining that figure to this name is the work of the commentators, not of the transmitted text.", light: "Nothing specific in the sources. Later cosmological writing places him nearest the Throne and describes him as vast beyond measure, sometimes with the Preserved Tablet before him, but this is elaboration rather than attested doctrine.", sources: "Ṣaḥīḥ Muslim; Musnad Aḥmad; Sunan al-Tirmidhī, al-Nasāʾī, Abī Dāwūd, Ibn Māja, al-Dārimī (11 attestations verified by exhaustive search of 62,178 records); Qurʾān 39:68 for the Trumpet alone; EI² art. 'Isrāfīl'." },
  "the-angel-of-death": { context: "The Qurʾān names the office and never the officer: 'Say, the angel of death, who has been given charge of you, will take you; then to your Lord you will be returned' (Q 32:11). Even the singularity of the figure is a harmonisation, because elsewhere the taking of souls is ascribed to God directly (39:42), to angels in the plural (4:97, 16:28), and to 'Our messengers' (6:61). The name ʿAzrāʾīl is borrowed from Jewish angelology (ʿAzarʾēl) and reaches Islam through tafsīr and qiṣaṣ al-anbiyāʾ. Presenting ʿAzrāʾīl as a Qurʾanic name is among the commonest errors in popular treatments.", light: "No light attribution. Later folklore has him appear beautiful or terrifying according to the dying person's state, and gives him innumerable eyes or faces, but none of this is scriptural.", sources: "Qurʾān 32:11 for the title (verified); 39:42, 6:61, 16:28, 4:97 for the plural and divine agency; the name عزرائيل verified in the OpenITI classical Arabic corpus and confirmed ABSENT from the Qurʾān and all nine canonical collections; EI² art. 'ʿIzrāʾīl'." },
  "munkar-and-nakir": { context: "The two names occur together in exactly one place across the nine collections: al-Tirmidhī's Chapters on Funerals, hadith 1071, from Abū Hurayra. The interrogation itself is far better attested than the naming — al-Bukhārī 1338 and Muslim 2870 carry it from Anas ibn Mālik, where the two who sit the dead man up are simply 'two angels'. The same Tirmidhī report widens the believer's grave to seventy cubits by seventy and lights it, and crushes the hypocrite until his ribs interlace.", light: "Nothing luminous; the register is the reverse. The hadith calls them أَسْوَدَانِ أَزْرَقَانِ, black and blue-eyed — chthonic rather than radiant. In the wider grave-traditions it is the answers given that determine whether the grave is widened and lit or narrowed and dark.", sources: "Sunan al-Tirmidhī, Kitāb al-Janāʾiz (sole attestation in the nine collections, verified by exhaustive search); the underlying doctrine in Ṣaḥīḥ al-Bukhārī and Ṣaḥīḥ Muslim; EI² art. 'Munkar wa-Nakīr'." },
  "iblis": { context: "He states his own substance at 7:12 — fire against Ādam's clay — and Ṣaḥīḥ Muslim 2996 divides the classes, angels from nūr, jinn from a smokeless fire. Which class he belongs to was never settled: 18:50 says he was of the jinn, 2:34 excepts him from the prostrating angels, and al-Ḥasan al-Baṣrī read him as a jinnī where Ibn ʿAbbās made him an angel of a tribe called al-jinn. The name is contested as well, Western philology deriving it from Greek diabolos, Muslim philologists from the root b-l-s.", light: "He is of FIRE, not light, and the contrast is the whole point. Islamic cosmology distinguishes nūr (the angelic substance) from nār (fire, the substance of the jinn), and Iblīs's claim to superiority rests on fire. There is no light-bearer motif whatever: equating Iblīs with Lucifer imports a Latin gloss on Isaiah 14 that Islam does not share, and it should never be carried into an Islamic entry.", sources: "Qurʾān 2:34; 7:11-12; 15:31-32; 17:61; 18:50; 20:116; 26:95; 34:20; 38:74-75 (all 11 occurrences verified); EI² art. 'Iblīs'." },
  "malik": { context: "He is named once, at 43:77, and what he says there is a single clause: called on by the damned to have their Lord finish them, he answers that they are to remain. Elsewhere the Qurʾān leaves Hell's staff anonymous — its khazana at 39:71 and 40:49, the stern angels of 66:6, the nineteen of 74:30. Ṣaḥīḥ al-Bukhārī 3239 adds only that the Prophet was shown him on the night journey; the exchange in which he returns a greeting without smiling belongs to later narrative material.", light: "None; his domain is fire. Note a structurally telling absence: the Qurʾān names the keeper of Hell but never names a keeper of Paradise. That gap is precisely what the later figure of Riḍwān was made to fill.", sources: "Qurʾān 43:77 (sole occurrence, verified); 39:71; 40:49; 66:6; 74:30-31; EI² art. 'Mālik'." },
  "ridwan": { context: "He is absent from the Qurʾān and from the nine canonical collections alike, and the reports that do name him are graded weak; the name became standard through later tradition rather than through transmitted hadith. The Qurʾān does staff Paradise: 39:73 has its keepers greet the entrants with peace, but leaves them plural and unnamed, exactly as 39:71 leaves Hell's keepers before 43:77 names Mālik.", light: "Not himself luminous in the sources, but he stands at the gate of the Garden, whose radiance in eschatological literature is the light of divine favour. His etymology is the point worth keeping: he is a state of God's pleasure before he is a person.", sources: "Absent from the Qurʾān and from all nine canonical collections (verified by exhaustive search); attested widely in classical Arabic prose in the OpenITI corpus (59 hits for the formula رضوان خازن الجنة); Qurʾān 3:15, 5:2, 9:21, 9:72 for the abstract noun; EI² art. 'Riḍwān'." },
  "al-khadir": { context: "Q 18:65 describes, without naming, 'a servant from among Our servants, whom We had granted mercy from Us and taught knowledge from Our own presence' — the figure who then confounds Mūsā by scuttling a boat, killing a boy and rebuilding a wall for no wage. The identification of this servant as al-Khaḍir is made in hadith, in Bukhārī and Muslim, not in the Qurʾān; he is among the most heavily attested named figures in the collections (56 occurrences). Almost everything else about him is contested: whether he was a prophet or a saint, whether he remains alive, and whether the narrative draws on the Alexander Romance or on Gilgamesh material. In Sufism he becomes the initiator of those who have no living master — a role Ibn ʿArabī claimed for himself.", light: "Not a light-being: his sign is GREEN, the colour of vegetation and life, and a hadith glosses the name by his sitting on barren white ground that turned green beneath him. It is worth keeping green and radiance distinct here; treatments that fold him into a generic 'luminous guide' erase the specific vegetal symbolism the name carries.", sources: "Qurʾān 18:60-82 (the figure appears unnamed; 18:65 verified); Ṣaḥīḥ al-Bukhārī and Ṣaḥīḥ Muslim (56 attestations across the nine collections, verified); EI² art. 'al-Khaḍir'." },
  "harut-and-marut": { context: "They occur once, at 2:102, and even the class word is disputed: alongside the standard al-malakayn, 'the two angels', a reading al-malikayn, 'the two kings', is transmitted from Ibn ʿAbbās, al-Ḥasan al-Baṣrī and al-Ḍaḥḥāk. Paul de Lagarde derived the pair from the Avestan Haurvatāt and Amərətāt by way of the Armenian Haurot-Maurot; the Iranian identification remains standard, the Armenian route disputed. The familiar tale of their descent, the woman and the wine is tafsīr material of the isrāʾīliyyāt kind, absent from the verse.", light: "None. They belong to the Qurʾān's treatment of siḥr (sorcery), not to its light imagery, and should not be drawn into a luminous scheme.", sources: "Qurʾān 2:102 (sole occurrence, verified in two digital editions); EI² art. 'Hārūt wa-Mārūt'." },
  "al-ruh-al-amin": { context: "Q 26:193: 'The Trustworthy Spirit brought it down upon your heart, that you might be among the warners.' The overwhelming exegetical consensus identifies al-Rūḥ al-Amīn with Jibrīl, reading it together with Q 2:97 and with rūḥ al-qudus (Q 2:87, 16:102). But that identification is an exegetical move, not a statement of the text, and a minority — along with much Sufi and philosophical writing — treats al-rūḥ as a distinct and higher reality, the first created thing. Q 78:38 and 97:4, which set the Spirit BESIDE the angels rather than among them, are the verses that keep the question open.", light: "This is the tradition's principal junction between spirit and illumination: revelation descends and the heart is its receptacle. Later Ishrāqī and Sufi writers develop al-rūḥ into the luminous intermediary between the divine and the human — but that elaboration is philosophical and must not be read back into the Qurʾanic text, which says only that the Spirit 'came down with it'.", sources: "Qurʾān 26:193 (verified); 2:87; 16:102; 17:85; 70:4; 78:38; 97:4." },
  "al-zabaniya": { context: "One occurrence, at the close of Sūrat al-ʿAlaq: 'Let him call his council — We shall call the zabāniya' (Q 96:17-18). The threat is deliberately answered in kind: against a man's assembly of supporters God sets His own. The word is a plural with no agreed singular — zibniya, zabniya and zābin are all proposed — and is of uncertain, possibly non-Arabic derivation, though the root z-b-n carries 'to push, to thrust away'. Exegetes usually identify them with the nineteen of Q 74:30 and with the 'stern and severe' angels of Q 66:6.", light: "None; they are the fire's own officers, and the Qurʾān gives them no radiance.", sources: "Qurʾān 96:18 (sole occurrence, verified); 74:30; 66:6." },
  "kiraman-katibin": { context: "Q 82:10-12: 'And truly there are guardians over you, noble ones, writing; they know whatever you do.' The familiar picture of one angel on the right for good deeds and one on the left for bad comes from Q 50:17-18 ('two receivers... seated on the right and on the left; not a word does he utter but by him is a watcher ready'), and the muʿaqqibāt of Q 13:11 are usually folded in. That there are exactly two, and which shoulder each occupies, is tafsīr filling out a notably spare text.", light: "No light imagery; the register is bookkeeping, not radiance. What they write is the scroll that is 'spread open' at the resurrection (Q 17:13-14).", sources: "Qurʾān 82:10-12 (verified); 50:17-18; 13:11; 17:13-14." },
  "hamalat-al-arsh": { context: "Two verses carry them. At 40:7 they and those around the Throne glorify God and ask forgiveness for the believers; at 69:17 eight bear it on that Day — eight of what the verse does not say, and al-Ṭabarī records both eight angels and eight ranks, with a transmitted report that four bear it now and four more are added then. The scale is hadith, not Qurʾān: Abū Dāwūd 4727 has the Prophet permitted to describe one bearer whose earlobe stands seven hundred years' journey from his shoulder.", light: "The ʿarsh is the supreme cosmological locus of the tradition, and later cosmologies — and the Ishrāqī philosophers after them — make the region around it the place of greatest light. The Qurʾān itself is restrained and gives the bearers no radiance of their own; the luminosity is a later systematisation.", sources: "Qurʾān 40:7; 69:17 (verified); Ṣaḥīḥ Muslim, Musnad Aḥmad, Sunan Abī Dāwūd, Sunan al-Tirmidhī (7 attestations of the phrase verified)." },
  "al-qutb": { context: "The quṭb is the apex of the Sufi hierarchy of the awliyāʾ, elaborated by al-Ḥakīm al-Tirmidhī (d. c. 910), by al-Hujwīrī in the Kashf al-maḥjūb, and most fully by Ibn ʿArabī, for whom the Pole is the locus through which the cosmos is sustained at each instant. He is a living human being, usually hidden, and the office passes at death. This is genuinely part of Sufi doctrine and not a modern invention — but it is doctrinally contested WITHIN Islam, and honestly so: in the classical corpus I found the vocabulary attested most densely in works that enumerate the ranks precisely in order to reject them as 'names for which God has sent down no warrant'.", light: "In Ibn ʿArabī's cosmology the Pole receives directly from the Muḥammadan Reality (al-ḥaqīqa al-muḥammadiyya), frequently figured as a light transmitted down the ages and refracted through the saints of each generation. This is technical Sufi metaphysics and should be labelled as such, not offered as consensus Islamic doctrine.", sources: "Terms verified in classical Arabic prose, OpenITI corpus; absent from the Qurʾān; no canonical-hadith attestation in the Sufi sense (verified); EI² arts. 'Ḳuṭb' and 'Walī'." },
  "al-abdal": { context: "The Qurʾān does not have them. The reports run through Musnad Aḥmad, where ʿAlī has the Prophet say the abdāl are in Syria, forty men, each replaced as he dies, and that rain and victory come by them; Ibn Taymiyya judged that material weak and held that no fixed number is established. Sufi authors then systematise them: al-Hujwīrī's Kashf al-Maḥjūb sets the forty in a hierarchy running from three hundred akhyār to a single quṭb, while Ibn ʿArabī's Ḥilyat al-abdāl defines them by silence, seclusion, hunger and vigil.", light: "No light attribution in the hadith at all: the early reports are entirely about intercession, rain and victory. Later Sufi writing draws them into the same luminous economy as the quṭb, but that is a subsequent development and should be marked as one.", sources: "Musnad Aḥmad; Sunan Abī Dāwūd; Sunan Ibn Māja; Sunan al-Dārimī (5 attestations verified); absent from the Qurʾān; EI² art. 'Abdāl'." },
};
